"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2797] \"Perhaps, stealing the revolver from Colonel Eastbrook's drawer was fun. Slipping up to the empty room upstairs while talking about eggs and jam. Lubricating the second door so that it opened and closed smoothly and silently - that was fun too. Moving the table outside the door so that Phyllida's arrangement of flowers would stand out more prominently - that was interesting. It was all like a game, but what was to follow was no longer a game. Yes, she was very frightened... Dora Bunner was not wrong.\" [2798] \"After all, she carried out the plan, and everything went according to plan. At 6:30, she went out to 'round up the ducks', letting Scherz in, giving him a mask, cape, gloves, and a flashlight. By the time the clock struck six, everything was ready, and she stood at the table near the arcade, reaching for the cigarette box on the table. She did it so naturally. The host, Patrick, went to get the drink. And she - the hostess - was reaching for the cigarettes. She correctly deduced that everyone would look at the clock when it struck six. And that's exactly what happened. Only one person, loyal Dora, kept her eyes on her friend. When she was first questioned, she accurately described what Miss Blacklock was doing at the time, saying that Miss Blacklock picked up the vase of violets.\" [2799] \"She had previously damaged the lamp's wiring, leaving the copper wire almost exposed. The whole process took only a second. The cigarette box, the vase, and the small switch were all within reach. She picked up the vase, splashed water on the exposed wire, and turned on the lamp switch. Water is an excellent conductor of electricity, and the fuse blew.\" [2800] \"Just like that afternoon here,\" said Round. \"It really gave you a fright, didn't it, Aunt Jane?\" [2801] \"Yes, my dear. I've been worried about the lamp. I realized there were two table lamps, a pair, and one had been switched for the other - probably done at night.\" [2802] \"That's right,\" said Cora Darcy. \"The next morning, Fletcher checked the table lamps and found that they were as undamaged as the other lamps, with no damage to the wires or melting.\" [2803] \"I understand what Dora Bunner meant by saying she was a shepherdess the day before,\" said Miss Marple. \"But I followed her thinking and fell into this thinking error, thinking it was Patrick who did it. One interesting thing about Dora Bunner is that when she repeats what she hears, she is not reliable, she always exaggerates or distorts facts with her imagination, and her imagination is often wrong; but when she sees something, she describes it very accurately. She saw Letitia pick up the vase of violets -\"\n[2804] \"And she also saw what she described as a flash and a crackle,\" Cora Darcy interjected. [2805] \"Of course, dear Round, when you spilled water from the vase of Christmas roses on the table lamp wires, I immediately realized that only Miss Blacklock herself could have burned the lamp, because only she was closest to the table.\" [2806] \"I should have kicked myself,\" said Coraldrake. \"Miss Bunner even mentioned the table being scarred from heat because someone 'put a cigarette on it,' but there was no one smoking, and the violets died for lack of water\u2014a mistake by Letitia, who should have refilled the vase. But I suppose she thought no one would notice, and Miss Bunna easily convinced herself she hadn't refilled it to begin with.\" [2807] He went on. [2808] \"Certainly, she's easily influenced, and Miss Blacklock has taken advantage of that more than once. I believe Miss Bunna's suspicions of Patrick were also induced. \" [2809] \"Why pick on me?\" Patrick asked in a hurt tone. [2810] \"I don't think it was a deliberate suggestion, but it prevented Miss Bunna from suspecting Miss Blacklock as the mastermind behind this tragedy. Well, as we know, when the lights went out, everyone started screaming, and she slipped out the door she had previously oiled, coming up behind Mr. Rudy Scherz, who was playing with a flashlight, eagerly acting out his role. I don't think he realized she was behind him, wearing gardening gloves and holding a revolver. She waited for the flashlight to shine on the wall she needed to aim at, and then fired two shots. When he turned around in surprise, she pointed the gun at him and fired again. She dropped the revolver next to his body, then casually tossed the gloves onto the table in the hallway and returned to her place, where she had been standing before the lights went out. She cut herself on the ear, and I'm not quite sure how\u2014\"\n[2811] \"I think she used a fingernail file,\" said Miss Maple, \"just a nick on the earlobe would make a lot of blood. Of course, it's a good psychological tactic. The blood on her white dress made it look like she had been shot and almost died.\" [2812] \"Everything was going well,\" said Corrado, \"Dora Bonner insisted that Scherz had definitely shot Miss Blacklock, and that worked. Although not intentionally, Dora Bonner conveyed the impression that she had actually seen her friend injured. The case could have been closed with suicide or accidental death. And it was thanks to Miss Maple here that it wasn't.\" [2813] \"Ah, no, no,\" Miss Maple shook her head vigorously. \"All my modest efforts were purely coincidental. It was you, Inspector Corrado, who was dissatisfied with the conclusion. It was you who prevented the case from being closed.\" [2814] \"I was not entirely satisfied with the conclusion,\" said Corrado. \"I knew something was wrong, but I couldn't see where it was until you showed me the way. After that, Miss Blacklock was truly in trouble. I discovered that the second door had been tampered with. We had all assumed that what had happened was just one possibility, but we had no real evidence except for our deductions. The oiled door was the evidence. It was a lucky guess, and purely coincidental - I pulled the wrong handle.\" [2815] \"I think you were guided there, Inspector,\" said Miss Maple. \"But, to be fair, I'm getting old.\" [2816] \"The search resumed,\" Corcoran said, \"but this time it was different. We were now looking for someone with a motive to kill Letitia Blacklock.\" [2817] \"And there was someone with a motive,\" Miss Marple said, \"Miss Blacklock knew it. I think she recognized Philippa almost at first sight. Because among those allowed to enter Charlotte's private life, Sonia Goddard was one of the few. And as people get older - something you don't know, Inspector Corcoran - they remember faces they saw when they were young better than those they saw a year or two ago. Philippa was probably about the same age as Sonia when she was young, and she looked very much like her mother. It's strange, but I think that after recognizing Philippa, Charlotte was actually happy. She liked Philippa and wanted to treat her like a daughter. She thought she would take care of Philippa when she inherited the money, and she would treat her like a daughter. Philippa and Harry should live with her. She was happy to do a good deed. But once the inspector started asking questions and found a pair of 'Pip and Emma,' Charlotte became restless. She didn't want Philippa to be a scapegoat, and her whole plan was to make it look like a young criminal came to rob, and the criminal died in an accident. But then, with the oiled door being discovered, the whole plan changed. Besides Philippa - whom I know for sure doesn't know Julia's true identity - no one else could have had a motive to kill her. She made every effort to hide Philippa's true identity. When you asked her, she thought quickly and told you that Sonia was short and had dark skin, and then she took Letitia's photo and also took Sonia's photo from the album, so you couldn't notice any similarities between Philippa and Sonia.\" [2818] \"And to make me suspect Swetnam as Sonia,\" Corcoran said with disgust. [2819] \"My poor mother,\" Edmund whispered, \"a woman with an impeccable life, or so I always believed.\" [2820] \"But,\" Miss Marple continued, \"the real danger was Dora Bunner. Dora was getting more and more forgetful and talkative every day. I remember how Charlotte looked at her that day when we had tea. Do you know why? Dora called her Lois. We thought it was a slip of the tongue, but it scared Charlotte. So everything continued. Poor Dora went on talking. That day we had coffee at 'Bluebird,' and I had a very strange impression that Dora was talking about two people, not one, but of course she was talking about the same person. She would say her friend wasn't pretty but had character, and then describe her as a pretty and carefree girl. She talked about Letit's cleverness and success, but then said how sad her life was, quoting a poem about 'bravely bearing the pain.' But this didn't seem to fit Letitia's life. I think that when Charlotte walked into the coffee shop\n[2821] \"It is probably the conversation in the coffee shop that truly sealed Dora's fate \u2013 if you allow for such an exaggeration. But I believe the outcome is the same... because as long as Dora Bonner is alive, Charlotte's life is not safe. She loves Dora and does not want to kill her, but she sees no other way out. And I anticipate \u2013 as I mentioned to you about the case of Nurse Ellerton \u2013 that she convinces herself that this is almost an act of mercy. Poor Bonnie \u2013 she won't live long anyway, and it may be a painful death. It is strange that she tries to make Bonnie's last day as happy as possible. Birthday dinner \u2013 a special cake...\"\n[2822] \"The Death of Tasty.\" Philippa shivered. [2823] \"Yes, yes, very much like that... She tried to make her friend happy until the end... a dinner with all her favorite foods, no one saying anything to upset her. Then, the pills in the aspirin bottle, whatever they were. She placed the pills on her nightstand, so when Bonnie couldn't find her new bottle, she would have to go to Letty's room to get some, making it seem like the pills were specifically for Letty...\n[2824] \"As a result, Bonnie passed away peacefully in her sleep, and Charlotte felt secure again. However, she missed Dora Bunner, missed her love and loyalty, missed talking about the past with Dora... she wept bitterly on the day I delivered the note to Julian, and her grief was sincere, for she had killed her beloved friend...\"\n[2825] \"This is too terrible,\" Yuan Yuan said, \"terrible.\" [2826] \"Yet it's human nature,\" said Julian Harmon, \"people often forget that murderers are very human too.\" [2827] \"I know,\" said Miss Marple, \"people, usually deserve pity, and are also extremely dangerous. Especially someone like Charlotte Blacklock, who is weak and good-hearted. This is because once a weak person is really afraid, they can become cruel and lose all self-control out of fear.\" [2828] \"And what about Muggleton?\" asked Julian. [2829] \"Yes, poor Miss Muggleton. Charlotte must have overheard them rehearsing the murder when she went to the cottage. The windows were open, and she just listened. Before that, she never thought there was anyone else who could be a threat. Miss Chippchase encouraged her friend to recall what she saw, but before that, Charlotte thought it was impossible for anyone to see the truth. She must have been listening at the window. Would it be a problem? Suddenly, just as Miss Chippchase rushed out to the police station, Miss Muggleton stumbled upon the truth. She called out behind her: 'She wasn't there\u2026'\u201d\n[2830] \"I asked Miss Chippchase about Miss Muggleton's way of saying that... because if she had said 'She wasn't there,' it would have been different.\" [2831] \"That's too subtle for me,\" said Cora Dedalus. [2832] Miss Maple's fair cheeks flushed, and she turned to him eagerly. [2833] \"Just imagine what Miss Muggleton was thinking... People often overlook things and don't realize them. There was once a railway accident, but I only remember a pool of paint on the side of the carriage, which I could still draw later. And once in London, a bomb fell from the sky, shattering glass everywhere, causing panic, but I remember most vividly a woman standing in front of me, with a hole in her stocking at the thigh, and the two stockings didn't match. So when Miss Muggleton stopped daydreaming and tried to recall what she had seen, she remembered a lot of things. She started recalling from the fireplace, with the flashlight shining first here, then following the light to the two windows, with someone in between her and the windows. For example, Mrs. Harmon had her hands over her eyes. Her mind followed the beam of light. Then her thoughts turned to the stunned Miss Bonner, an empty wall, a table with a lamp and a cigarette box, and then the gunshot \u2013 the most unbelievable thing in her memory. She saw the wall, and later there were two bullet holes in it, where Miss Blacklock leaned when she was shot, and Miss Letty was not there...\n[2834] \"Do you understand what I mean? Miss Chinchcliff asked her to recall where the three women were, and she focused on that. If one of them was not there, she could pinpoint that person and say, 'Ah, that's it! She wasn't there!' But what she remembered was the location \u2013 the place where someone should have been \u2013 but it was empty \u2013 no one was there. The place was still there, but the person was gone. She couldn't believe it. 'It's surprising, Chinch,' she said, 'she wasn't there...'\"\n[2835] \"I asked Miss Chinchcliff about the way Miss Muggleton said that phrase... because if she had said 'She wasn't there,' it would have meant something different.\" [2836] \"But you knew that before, didn't you?\" Round said, \"When the lamp burnt out, you wrote those things on the paper.\" [2837] \"Yes, my dear. All the clues came together, you see, all the fragmented and unrelated things formed a coherent pattern.\" [2838] Round quoted softly: \" 'Lamp? Yes. Violet? Yes. The bottle of aspirin.' You mean that on the day Bonnie bought a new bottle, she didn't need to take Letty's? \" [2839] \"Unless her own bottle was taken or hidden by someone. It looked like someone wanted to kill Letty Blacklock.\" [2840] \"Yes, I see. 'Death by Delight.' It was the cake, not just the cake. The whole dinner was a trap to make Bonnie happy for a day and then die. Treating her like a dog to be put down. What I find most terrifying is this - a false sense of compassion.\" [2841] \"She was a good woman. She was telling the truth in the kitchen: 'I don't want to kill anyone.' She craved money that didn't belong to her. This desire - it became an obsession - to use that money to compensate for all the suffering life had given her - had not been satisfied yet, and everything was lost. People who resent life are often dangerous, they seem to feel that life owes them too much. I know many disabled people who have suffered much more than Charlotte Blacklock and have been deprived of more by life. One's happiness and misery depend on oneself. But oh, dear, I digress, where were we?\" [2842] \"To your list,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"What did you mean by 'consult'?\" [2843] Miss Marple nodded mischievously at Inspector Corrigan. [2844] \"You must have seen this, Inspector Corrigan. You showed me the letter Letitia Blacklock wrote to her sister. The word 'consult' appeared twice, and each time it was spelled with an e. But in the note I had Yuan Yuan give you, Miss Blacklock wrote 'consult' with an i.\" [2845] \"Yes,\" agreed Inspector Corrigan. \"I should have noticed that.\" [2846] Round continued, \"Endure bravely the torture of pain.\" That's what Bonnie said to you in the caf\u00e9, Letitia certainly didn't suffer. And \"iodine\", did that clue lead you to think of thyroid enlargement? [2847] \"Yes, dear. You know, Switzerland, and Letitia Blacklock gave the impression that her 'sister' died of lung disease. But I remember that at the time, the most skilled and authoritative surgeon for thyroid enlargement was Swiss. This connects with Letitia Blacklock's peculiar necklace that she never took off. The jewelry wasn't in her usual style \u2013 it was perfect for concealing scars.\" [2848] \"Now I understand why she was so agitated when the necklace broke that night,\" said Colonel Corcoran. \"It seemed very unusual at the time.\" [2849] \"Later, you wrote 'Lotty' instead of the name we thought was Letitia,\" said Round. [2850] \"Yes, I remember that the sister's name was Charlotte. Dorothy Bunner once or twice called Miss Blacklock 'Lotty', and each time she did, she seemed uneasy.\" [2851] \"So what about Bern and the pension?\" [2852] \"Rudi worked as a janitor in a hospital in Bern.\" [2853] \"And the pension?\" [2854] \"Oh, my dear Yuan Yuan, I mentioned it to you at 'Bluebird', but only casually, without thinking about using it here. Mrs. Wutherspoon took Mrs. Butler's pension in addition to her own, but Mrs. Butler had been dead for years. Because the old ladies look so similar, yes, all this forms a pattern. I was so excited at the time that I went out to let my mind cool down and think about how to prove everything. Later, Miss Chinchcliffe picked me up, and we found Miss Muggleton...\"\n[2855] Miss Maple's tone lowered, and all the joy and excitement disappeared, leaving only calm. [2856] \"I knew I had to do something, and do it quickly. But there was no real evidence. So I came up with a feasible plan and told Chief Constable Fletcher.\" [2857] \"I gave Fletcher a good talking-to!\" said Colonel Draco. \"He had no right to agree to your plan without consulting me first.\" [2858] \"He didn't like it, but I convinced him,\" said Miss Marple. \"We went to the estate and found Micky.\" [2859] Julia drew in a sharp breath. \"I can't imagine how you managed to convince her.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nBut a woman and a man have been killed, and if I don't do something about it, another woman may be killed. I'm not thinking of Pip and Emma just now; I'm thinking of Sonya. I wish I knew what she looked like. There are a couple of snapshots in these letters, but none of them is likely to be her.\" [2232] \"How do you know it's not her? Do you know what she used to look like?\" [2233] \"She was short and dark, that's what Miss Blacklock said.\" [2234] \"Really?\" said Miss Maple. \"That's very interesting.\" [2235] \"There's one snapshot that reminds me of someone. It's a tall, pretty girl with her hair up on top of her head. I don't know who she could be. Anyway, it certainly isn't Sonya. Do you think Mrs. Swithin could have been dark when she was a girl?\" [2236] \"No, not very,\" said Round. \"She had blue eyes.\" [2237] \"I wish I had a photo of Dmitri Stanfield, but I suppose that's asking too much\u2026\" He picked up the letter. \"I'm sorry it hasn't given you any clues, Miss Marple.\" [2238] \"Oh, it has, indeed,\" said Miss Marple. \"Look at it again, Inspector, especially the bit about Randall Goodale investigating Dmitri Stanfield.\" [2239] Corrado stared at her. [2240] The telephone rang. [2241] Roundhound got up from the floor and went into the hall. Following the Victorian tradition, the phone was still there, and still there it remained. [2242] She returned to the living room and said to Kollerstrom, \"It's for you.\" [2243] The Inspector was a little surprised, went out to answer the phone - while carefully closing the living room door behind him. [2244] \"Kollerstrom? This is Ridestone.\" [2245] \"Yes, sir.\" [2246] \"I've gone through your report. When you talked to Felicia Hammers, she positively claimed that she hadn't seen her husband since he ran away from the army, is that right?\" [2247] \"Yes, sir. She said it very positively. But I think she's not telling the truth.\" [2248] \"I agree with your opinion. Do you remember the case ten days ago? A man was hit by a large truck and then sent to Milchester General Hospital, resulting in a concussion and pelvic fracture, do you remember?\" [2249] \"The one who saved the child from under the wheel and got hurt himself?\" [2250] \"That's the one. He had no identification and no one came to claim him. It looks like he was wanted by the police. He never regained consciousness and died last night. But his identity was established. He was a deserter named Ronald Hammers, who served as a captain in South Lom County.\" [2251] \"Phyllis Hyde's husband?\" [2252] \"Yes. He had an old ticket to Chipping Craighorne and, by the way, a lot of money.\" [2253] \"So he did get money from his wife. I always thought he was the one talking to Phyllis in the gazebo. Of course, she denied it. Sir, the accident happened before\u2014\"\n[2254] Ridestad interrupted him:\n[2255] \"Yes. He was admitted to Milchester General Hospital on the 28th, and the robbery at the small farm took place on the 29th. This rules out any possibility of his being involved in the incident. However, his wife, of course, doesn't know about the accident. She's keeping quiet, which is natural, as he is her husband.\" [2256] \"Truly a heroic act of bravery, isn't it, sir?\" Corradino said slowly. [2257] \"Saving a child from under the wheels? Yeah, that's something. Don't think Hammers fled from the army out of cowardice. But that's all in the past now. For a man who ruined his own reputation, it's a fitting end.\" [2258] \"I am happy for her,\" said the Inspector, \"and for their son as well.\" [2259] \"Yes, he need not feel too ashamed of his father. The young woman can remarry again.\" [2260] Kordok slowly said, \"I was also thinking about this, Director... this shows... possibilities.\" [2261] \"Since you were on the scene, it would be best for you to deliver the news.\u201d\n[2262] \"I will, Chief. I'll go right away. Perhaps it would be best to wait until she returns to the small enclosure. The news might be quite shocking, and besides, I'd like to talk to someone else first.\" [2263] ** ** **\n[2264] [1] Charlotte's first letter is C.\n[2265] Chapter Nineteen: Recap of the Case\n[2266] 1\n[2267] \"I'll get a lamp and put it on the side for you, and then I'll leave,\" said Round Round. \"It's quite dark here. I think a storm is coming.\" [2268] She picked up the small reading lamp and put it on the other side of the table, so that the light would shine on Miss Marple as she knitted her sweater. She was sitting in a large high-backed chair. [2269] The wire was run across the table, and the cat leaped up onto the table and began biting and clawing at the wire. [2270] \"Don't move, Tigris, don't\u2026 It's terrible. Look, it's almost bitten through the wire, it's all broken. Don't you know? You silly little cat, you'll get electrocuted if you do that.\" [2271] \"Thank you, dear,\" said Miss Maple, reaching out to turn on the light. [2272] \"Not that one. You have to press the small switch in the middle of the wire. Wait a moment, I'll take these flowers away so they don't get in the way.\" [2273] She picked up a bottle of Christmas roses from the other end of the table. Tiggy wagged his tail and suddenly extended a mischievous paw, scratching Yuan Yuan's arm. She splashed some water from the vase onto the wires that Tiggy had bitten and onto Tiggy himself, who growled angrily and jumped off the table. [2274] Miss Maple pressed the small pear-shaped switch. A spark burst from the spot where the wires had been bitten and wet with water. [2275] \"Oh, dear,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"The fuse has blown. Now I guess all the lights should be off. \" She tried each switch. \"Yes, they're all off. So, all the lines are connected to this small device, how stupid. And it's burned a spot on the table. It's all Tiggy's fault, the mischievous one. Auntie Jane, what's wrong? Are you scared?\" [2276] \"No, dear. I just happened to notice something I should have seen before\u2026\"\n[2277] \"I'll go change the fuse and then bring the table lamp from Julian's study.\" [2278] \"No, dear, don't bother. You'll miss your bus. I don't need the light anymore, I just want to sit here and think. Go on, dear, or you'll miss your bus.\" [2279] After Miss Marple left, she sat quietly for a couple of minutes. The air in the room was heavy and damp, indicating an approaching storm. [2280] Miss Marple moved a piece of paper in front of her. [2281] She wrote down: \"Lamp?\" and drew a thick line underneath. [2282] After a while, she wrote down another word. [2283] Her pen glided across the paper, scribbling down brief, cryptic phrases\u2026\n[2284] 2\n[2285] The drawing room at Grasmere Hall had low ceilings and stained-glass windows that cast a dim light. Miss Quincannon and Miss Muggleton were arguing inside. [2286] \"Your problem, Mulgrew,\" said Miss Quincannon, \"is that you won't try.\" [2287] \"I tell you, Quincannon, I don't remember anything.\" [2288] \"Well, listen here, Amy Mulgrew, we need to do some constructive thinking. So far, we haven't made any progress in the investigation. I was wrong about the door. After all, you didn't hold it open for the killer. You're innocent, Mulgrew!\" [2289] Miss Mulgrew smiled faintly. [2290] \"It's fortunate that we have a cleaning woman who knows how to keep quiet, Chipping Chilcote. I usually appreciate that, but this time, she's put us in a bad position. Everyone knows that the second door in the living room was used, and we didn't know until yesterday...\"\n[2291] \"I'm still not quite clear \u2014\"\n[2292] \"It's simple. Our original assumption was correct: you couldn't have kept the door open while holding a flashlight and a revolver in your hand. We kept the revolver and the flashlight, but we should have omitted the revolver. We made a mistake.\" [2293] \"But he did have a revolver,\" said Miss Mulgrew. \"I saw it on the ground next to him.\" [2294] \"He didn't use it when he was alive \u2014\"\n[2295] \"Then who fired the shot?\" [2296] \"We're looking for that person. But whoever fired the shot, the same person put two poisoned aspirins on Miss Blacklock's bedside table, which killed poor Dora Bunner. And it couldn't have been Rudi Schlesinger, because he was dead as a doornail. It was the person in the living room on the night of the robbery, and that person may have been at the birthday party. The only one who wasn't there was Mrs. Harmon.\" [2297] \"You think someone put poisoned aspirins there on the night of the birthday party?\" [2298] \"Why not?\" [2299] \"But how could they do that?\" [2300] \"Well, we all went to the bathroom, didn't we?\" said Miss Inchcliff gruffly. \"I washed my hands because the cake was so sticky. And Miss Eastbrooke was powdering Miss Blacklock's face in the bathroom, wasn't she?\" [2301] \"Chin! You think it was her? \u2014\"\n[2302] \"I don't know. If she did, it would be too obvious. If you wanted to put the pills there, I don't think you'd want someone to see you in the bathroom, would you? There are plenty of other opportunities.\" [2303] \"The men didn't go upstairs.\" [2304] \"There's the back staircase. Besides, if a man left the house, you wouldn't follow him around to make sure he really went where you did, would you? That would be undignified! Anyway, don't argue with me, Miss Mulgrew. I want to start over from the first murder of Letty Blacklock. Now, first of all, remember the facts, because it all depends on you.\" [2305] Miss Mulgrew looked tense. [2306] \"Oh, dear, Chippy, you know I'm all mixed up about all this! -\"\n[2307] \"The problem is not with your brain, or with the gray cells you call your brain. The problem is with your eyes; the problem is what you saw at the time. -\"\n[2308] \"But I saw nothing. -\"\n[2309] \"As I said, your trouble is, Murgatroyd, you don't try hard enough. Now, here's what happened that night: whoever went to kill Letty Blacklock that night was in the house. He - I say he, because it's more convenient to say, but there's no reason to assume it was a man and not a woman, except that men are generally scoundrels - anyway, he oiled the door from the living room to the outside, which should have been nailed shut. Don't ask me when he did it, because that would muddy the waters. In fact, if I had to pick a time, I could walk into any pub in Chipping Clegg-horn and do anything I wanted in about half an hour without anyone noticing. Find out where the staff is, when the owner goes out, where exactly he's going, and for how long. Now, let's think, he oiled the second door so that it wouldn't make a sound when opened. The plan was: lights out, door A - the front door - opened with a bang. Flash a flashlight and say the word \"robbery\". Meanwhile, while we were all gaping, X - that's the best way to call him - slipped quietly from door B into the hallway, came up behind the Swiss idiot, and shot Letty Blacklock twice before shooting the Swiss. Then he dropped the gun. So only someone like you, who doesn't like to think, would think that was evidence of the Swiss firing the gun. Then he quickly made his way back to the living room while we were looking for a lighter. Understand? -\"\n[2310] \"Yes, yes, but who was it? -\"\n[2311] \"Well, if you don't know, Muggleton, then nobody does!\" [2312] \"Me?\" Muggleton exclaimed in surprise. \"I don't know. I really don't, Quinn!\" [2313] \"Use that thing you call a brain. First, where was everyone when the lights went out?\" [2314] \"I don't know.\" [2315] \"No, you do. You're just muddled, Muggleton. You know where you were yourself, don't you? You were behind the door.\" [2316] \"Yes, yes, I was behind the door. I bumped my toe when the door opened.\" [2317] \"Why can't you go see a proper foot doctor, instead of messing around with it? You're going to get gangrene one of these days. So, you were behind the door, I was standing by the fireplace, and I was just about to have a drink. Letty Blacklock was at the table in the arcade, reaching for a cigarette. Patrick went through the arcade to the small sitting room to get the drink Letty Blacklock had left there. Agreed?\" [2318] \"Yes, yes. I remember all that.\" [2319] \"Good. Now, someone was following Patrick through the small sitting room, or was about to go with him, a man. The question is, I can't remember whether it was Eastbrooke or Edmund Swetnam. Do you remember?\" [2320] \"No, I don't.\" [2321] \"You just can't remember! Someone else went to the small parlor, it was Philippa Hemmes. I remember this very well because I remember noticing how beautiful her straight back was, and I thought to myself: 'The girl would look good riding a horse.' I was looking at her, thinking about that. She walked to the fireplace in the small parlor, I don't know what she went there for, because just then the lights went out. [2322] \"That's how everyone was positioned: Patrick Simmons was in the living room, Philippa Hemmes was there, and either Eastbrook Colonel or Edmund Swetnam - but I don't know who. Now, Muggleton, pay attention, the most likely culprit is one of these three. Anyone who wanted to get out of the door from a distance would have to be in a convenient position to act as soon as the lights went out. So I say the most likely culprit is one of these three. If that's the case, Muggleton, you're out of luck!\" [2323] It was clear that Miss Muggleton's face lit up. [2324] \"On the other hand,\" Miss Chinchcliffe continued, \"it may not be any of the three. That's where you come in, Muggleton.\" [2325] \"But how could I know the situation at the time?\" [2326] \"I've already said that if you don't know, then no one does.\" [2327] \"But I don't know! I really don't know! I couldn't see anything!\" [2328] \"Oh, no, you could see. You were the only