hat Miss Blacklock threw away half a bottle of sherry\u2014opened a new one. I guess she wouldn't treat an opened bottle of aspirin the same way. Who was in the house\u2014in the last couple of days? These pills couldn't have been there for long.\" [1981] Ridley looked at him. [1982] \"Everyone was there yesterday,\" he said, \"for Miss Bunner's birthday party. Any one of them could have slipped up to the room and switched the pills without anyone noticing. Of course, anyone living in the house could have done it at any time.\" [1983] Chapter 17: Shadows of the Past\n[1984] Miss Marple stood at the door of the vicarage, wrapped up in her coat, and took the note from the round hand. [1985] \"Tell Miss Blacklock,\" said the round hand, \"that Julian couldn't come himself, and he's very sorry. There's a parishioner in the village who's dying. If Miss Blacklock would like to see him, he'll come over after lunch. The note is about the funeral arrangements. If the investigation is on Tuesday, he suggests the funeral on Wednesday. Poor old Bonnie, somehow she took the poisoned aspirin that was meant for someone else, and that's just like her. Goodbye, dear, and I hope you won't be too tired. But I really must take the child to the hospital right away.\" [1986] Miss Marple replied that it was not too far for her, and the round voice hastily left. [1987] While waiting for Miss Blacklock, Miss Marple looked around the room, wondering what Pat had meant by saying that he had \"tampered with the lamp\" so that \"all the lights went out.\" What lamp? How had he tampered with it? [1988] Miss Marple concluded that he must have meant the lamp on the table by the arch. She had mentioned the shepherdess or shepherd boy - actually a delicate piece of Dresden ware, a shepherd boy in a blue coat and pink trousers holding a lamp - which was originally a candlestick and had been adapted to electricity. The shade was made of pure sheepskin, rather too large, almost covering the figure of t