at's it! And one more thing, Mrs. Swetnam wears a wig. At least, that's what Mrs. Harmon said.\" [2056] \"Yes, yes, I suppose it could have been a wig, those little gray curls. But I still think it's absurd. She was such a nice person, and sometimes quite amusing.\" [2057] \"And then there's Miss Inchcliff and Miss Mulgrew. Which one of them do you think could be Sonia Godley?\" [2058] \"Miss Inchcliff is too tall. She's as tall as a man.\" [2059] \"And what about Miss Mulgrew?\" [2060] \"Oh, no, I don't think Miss Mulgrew could be Sonia. No, I'm sure she couldn't be.\" [2061] \"Your eyesight isn't very good, is it, Miss Blacklock?\" [2062] \"You mean I'm short-sighted?\" [2063] \"Yes. I'd like to see a snapshot of Miss Goddard, even if it's an old one and probably doesn't look like her now. You know, we've been trained professionally to find similarities, and that's something an amateur couldn't do.\" [2064] \"I'll try to find it for you.\" [2065] \"Can you do it right now?\" [2066] \"What, immediately?\" [2067] \"I hope you can find it now.\" [2068] \"Okay. Let me think. There are so many books in the cabinet. When we were clearing the books, I saw that album. Julia helped me with it. I remember she laughed at the clothes we wore back then... we moved the books to the shelves in the living room. Where did we put those albums and a bunch of Art Magazines? My memory is terrible! Maybe Julia will remember, she's at home today.\" [2069] \"I'll find her.\" [2070] The inspector ended the questioning. He didn't find Julia in any of the rooms downstairs. And Midge, when asked where Miss Simmons went, said it wasn't her business. [2071] \"It's me again! I was in my kitchen, worrying about lunch. Everything I ate was made by myself. Not one thing was not. Did you hear that?\" [2072] The inspector shouted upstairs, \"Miss Simmons!\" but there was no response, so he went up the stairs. [2073] At the turn of the stairs, he almost bumped into Julia. She had just come out of a door, and behind it was a winding staircase. [2074] \"I was in the attic,\" she explained. \"What's up?\" [2075] Inspector Corradini explained himself. [2076] \"Those old albums? Yes, I remember very well. I think we put the albums in a big cabinet in the study. I'll go and get them for you.\" [2077] She led him downstairs and opened the door of the study. There was a large cabinet near the window. [2078] \"Junk,\" said Julia, \"All junk. But old people just won't throw them away.\" [2079] Inspector Corradino knelt on the ground and took out two old-fashioned albums from the bottom drawer. [2080] \"Are these the ones?\" [2081] \"Yes.\" [2082] Miss Blacklock entered the room and joined them. [2083] \"Oh, I forgot where we put them. I don't even remember.\" [2084] Coleridge placed the albums on the table and began flipping through them. [2085] There were pictures of women wearing large wheel hats, skirts trailing the ground and making it hard for them to walk. The captions underneath were neatly written, but the ink had faded over time. [2086] \"It should be in this one,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"Probably on the second or third page. The other one was taken after Sonia got married and left.\" [2087] \"It should be here,\" she stopped. [2088] There were several blank spaces on the page. Corrado lowered his head to read the faded words. [2089] \"Sonia... I... Randall Goodale.\" The next line read \"Sonia and Bella at the beach.\" The opposite page had \"A picnic in Skane.\" He turned to another page. \"Charlotte, me, and Randall Goodale.\" [2090] Corrado stood up, his lips set in a stern line. [2091] \"Someone took the photos \u2013 I'd say, not long ago.\" [2092] \"There were no blank spaces when we looked at it, right, Julia?\" [2093] \"I didn't look closely \u2013 I was just noticing their clothes. No... you're right, Aunt Letty, there were no blank spaces.\" [2094] Corrado's expression grew colder. [2095] \"Someone,\" he said, \"has taken all the photos of Sonya out o