s, thank you, Letty,\" said Miss Bunce. \"I need to stop by the pharmacy for some aspirin and a blister plaster.\" [1605] After the door to \"The Bluebird\" closed behind them, Round asked, \"What were you talking about?\" [1606] Miss Marple didn't answer right away. After she had finished her tea, she said, \"Family ties are a very powerful thing. Very powerful. Do you remember that famous case? I can't remember which one it was. They said the husband poisoned the wife, and put the poison in a glass of wine. At the trial, the daughter said she drank half of her mother's glass - which negated the charge against the father. They did say - though perhaps it was just a rumor - that after that, she never spoke to her father again and never lived with him again. Of course, a father is one thing, but a nephew or cousin is another. But it's still the same - no one wants their family to be hanged, right?\" [1607] \"Yes,\" Round thought for a moment. \"I don't think they would.\" [1608] Miss Marple leaned back in her chair and murmured to herself, \"People are so alike, no matter where they are.\" [1609] \"Who do I look like?\" [1610] \"Well, dear, to be honest, you look like yourself. I can't think of anyone else you remind me of, except perhaps -\"\n[1611] \"You're doing it again,\" said Round Round. [1612] \"I was just thinking of one of my former parlour maids, dear.\" [1613] \"I'd be a terrible maid,\" said Round Round. [1614] \"Yes, dear, she was too. She wasn't very good at serving others at the table. The table was always a mess, the kitchen knives mixed with the dining room knives, and her hat - this was a long time ago - she never wore it properly.\" [1615] Round Round unconsciously adjusted her hat. [1616] \"What happened next?\" she asked eagerly. [1617] \"I kept her on because she was such good company and always made me laugh. I liked her straightforward way of talking. One day she said to me, 'Of course, I don't know, ma'am,' she said, 'but Miss Florence's posture is just like a married woman.' Poor Miss Florence did have trouble \u2013 she fell in love with a gentleman from the hair salon who was very polite. I spoke to him, and they had a very nice wedding and settled down happily. Miss Florence was a good girl, but she was easily attracted to polite men.\" [1618] \"Did she do anything criminal?\" asked Yuan Yuan. \"I mean, the parlor maid.\" [1619] \"No, really,\" said Miss Marple. \"She married a Baptist minister, and they had three children.\" [1620] \"Just like me,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"Although so far, I only have Edward and Susan.\" [1621] After a moment, she added, \"Who are you thinking of, Aunt Jane?\" [1622] \"Many people, dear, many people,\" Miss Marple said evasively. [1623] \"In St. Mary Mead?\" [1624] \"Mostly, I suppose... I thought of Nurse Eaton \u2013 a truly remarkable, kind woman. She took care of an elderly lady who seemed to really like her. Then the lady died. And then another lady, and she died too. It turned out she had been administering morphine. In the kindest way possible, it was truly horrifying, that the woman didn't even feel she had done wrong. 'They weren't going to live long anyway,' she said. One had cancer, quite painful.\" [1625] \"You mean \u2013 it was a merciful murder?\" [1626] \"No, no. They left her money in their wills. She did it for the money, you know...\"\n[1627] \"Then there was the young man on the liner\u2014Pussycat's nephew from the paper shop. He brought home things he had stolen, said he bought them abroad, and she believed him. Then the police came and questioned him, and he pinned it all on her, so she couldn't get out of it... He wasn't a good man\u2014but he was good-looking, and two women fell in love with him. He spent a lot of money on one of them.\" [1628] \"I think that was the dirtiest one,\" said Round Round. [1629] \"Yes, dear. And then there was Mrs. Crewe from the wool shop, who doted on her son and spoiled him rotten. He got mixed up with a bad crowd. Do you remember Joan Crowfoot, Round Round?\" [1630] \"No, I don't.\" [1631] \"I think you met her when I took you around, always with a cigarette or a pipe in her mouth, striding around. There was a bank robbery, and Joan Crowfoot was there at the time. She knocked the man down and took his revolver. The judge commended her bravery. [1632] Round listened intently, as if committing everything to memory. [1633] \"And?\" she asked. [1634] \"That summer, there was a girl from Saint-Jean-de-Collins \u2013 such a quiet girl \u2013 not quiet in the sense of being silent, but everyone liked her, but not very well. We heard her husband was a forger, which made her feel isolated by people. It changed her, you know, depression can change a person. [1635] \"Do you remember any British colonels who served in India, dear?\" [1636] \"Of course, dear. There's a Major Vance at Rajes, and a Colonel Wright in Shimla. They're fine. But I do remember Mr. Hodgson, who went on a long journey and married a young woman who could have been his daughter. I don't know where she came from \u2013 except what she told him.\" [1637] \"And she wasn't telling the truth?\" [1638] \"No, dear, she definitely wasn't.\" [1639] \"Not bad,\" Round nodded, counting people on her fingers, \"we have the whole-hearted Dorla, the dashing Patrick, Mrs. Sweetnam, Edmund, Philippa Hemmes, Colonel Eastbrook and his wife \u2013 if I may say so, your opinion about Dorla is spot on. But she had no reason to murder Letitia Blacklock.\" [1640] \"There may be something Letitia Blacklock knew but didn't want others to know.\" [1641] \"Oh, dear, just the usual old stuff? That must be ancient history.\" [1642] \"Perhaps not. You see, Round Round, you're not the type to care what people think of you.\" [1643] \"I see what you mean,\" Round Round said suddenly. \"When you've been through a lot of hard times, like a lost cat shaking with cold, and then you find a home, a warm hand to stroke you, and people call you a pretty kitty and care for you wholeheartedly\u2026 to keep all that, you'd do anything. Well, I must say, you've shown me all sorts of people.\" [1644] \"But you don't see them clearly.\" Miss Marple said gently. [1645] \"Do I miss anything? Julia? Julia, the lovely Julia is so odd.\" [1646] \"Three shillings and sixpence,\" the waitress said, coming out of the shadows. [1647] \"And besides,\" she went on, her chest heaving under the \"bluebird\" on her uniform, \"I want to know, Mrs. Harmon, why you say I'm peculiar. I have a cousin who's one of the 'peculiar ones', but I've always been a Church of England myself, and retired Hopkins can tell you that.\" [1648] \"I'm really sorry,\" round round said. \"I was just quoting a song. I didn't mean you. I didn't know your name was Julia.\" [1649] \"Well, that's quite a coincidence,\" the waitress said, her attitude softening. \"I believe you didn't mean to offend, but hearing my name\u2026 well, it's natural, isn't it? If you think someone is talking about you, it's human nature to listen. Thank you.\" [1650] She took the tip and left. [1651] \"But it must be,\" Miss Marple murmured, \"it can't be like this. It doesn't make sense -\"\n[1652] \"Auntie!\" said Roundhay. [1653] Miss Marple sighed and smiled brightly. [1654] \"Nothing, dear,\" she said. [1655] \"Do you think you know who the murderer is?\" asked Roundhay. \"Who is it?\" [1656] \"I haven't the slightest idea,\" replied Miss Marple. \"I suddenly had an idea\u2014but it disappeared. I wish I knew. Time is so short, so very short.\" [1657] \"What do you mean by short?\" [1658] \"The Scottish old lady could die at any moment.\" [1659] Yuan Yuan's eyes widened. \"So you really believe in Pip and Emma, then? You think they did it\u2014and they'll do it again?\" [1660] \"They'll certainly do it again,\" said Miss Marple almost absent-mindedly. \"Once you've made up your mind to kill someone, you don't give up because you've failed the first time. Especially when you're sure you haven't been suspected.\" [1661] \"But if it's Pip and Emma,\" said Round Round, \"there are only two possibilities. It must be Patrick and Julia. They're brother and sister, and their ages fit.\" [1662] \"It's not so simple, my dear. There are all sorts of possibilities and combinations. There's Pip's wife\u2014if he's married\u2014or Emma's husband. There's their mother\u2014even if she can't inherit directly, she's interested. If Miss Blacklock hasn't seen her for thirty years, she may not recognize her now. Older women all look alike. You remember Mrs. Waters, who collected her own pension and Mrs. Butler's as well, even though Mrs. Butler had been dead for years. And Miss Blacklock is myopic. Have you noticed how she looks at people? Then there's their father, who's obviously a bad lot.\" [1663] \"Yes, but he's a foreigner.\" [1664] \"That's true, but there's no reason to believe that he speaks English with an accent or that he gestures wildly when he talks. I dare say he's playing the part of\u2014a British army officer serving in India, and doing it just as well as anyone else.\" [1665] \"Is that your idea?\" [1666] \"No, not really, dear. I just thought that a large sum of money is in danger, a large sum indeed. I know only too well that people will do terrible things for a large sum of money.\" [1667] \"I think they would, but it wouldn't benefit them, would it? There would be repercussions?\" [1668] \"Yes\u2014but they usually don't think that way.\" [1669] \"I can understand that.\" The Roundhead suddenly smiled, a sweet, crooked smile. \"Everyone feels differently about money\u2026 even me. I've been thinking, 'You'll get that money, and you'll use it for all sorts of good things. Make plans\u2026 provide a home for abandoned children. Send hard-working women to a foreign country to rest and relax after a lifetime of toil\u2026'\"\n[1670] Her expression turned somber, and her eyes suddenly looked sad and melancholy. [1671] \"I know what you're thinking,\" she said to Miss Marple, \"you think I would be the worst kind of person because I have children. If it's just for selfish reasons that you want that money, you would feel ashamed. But once you pretend that you're using the money for good deeds, you can convince yourself that maybe killing isn't such a big deal...\"\n[1672] Then her eyes brightened again. [1673] \"But I can't do it,\" she said. \"I can't bring myself to do it. Not even to old people, sick people, or people who have done terrible things in the world. Even if it's someone who is cheating or \u2013 or a real beast, it's not okay.\" [1674] She blew gently on the fly. It moved its legs and then wobbled away. [1675] \"Cheer up, dear Aunt Jane,\" said Yuan Yuan. \"I would never kill anyone.\" [1676] Chapter 14: Looking Back\n[1677] After a night on the train, Inspector Cordiner alighted at a small station in the Scottish Highlands. [1678] For a moment, he was surprised that the wealthy Mrs. Goddard \u2014 a disabled woman \u2014 had chosen to live in the remote Scottish home instead of the fashionable square in London, the Hampshire estate, or the villa in the south of France. She must have cut herself off from many friends and entertainment. This must be a lonely life \u2014 or was she so sick that she couldn't notice or care about her surroundings anymore? [1679] A car waited to pick him up, an old-fashioned, spacious Daimler driven by an elderly driver. It was a sunny morning, and during the twenty-mile drive, the inspector enjoyed himself despite being surprised by this choice for seclusion. A casual remark opened the driver's floodgates, giving him a rough understanding of the reason behind the decision. [1680] \"It's her home before she got married. Oh, she's the last one of the family. She was happier here with Mr. Goddard than anywhere else, even though he couldn't often get away from London. But whenever he came, they were like two children. Happy.\" [1681] As the ancient mansion's gray walls came into view, Inspector Corrado felt as though time had reversed. An elderly male butler greeted him, and after freshening up, the inspector was led to a room with a roaring fireplace, where he had breakfast. [1682] After breakfast, a middle-aged nurse dressed in a nurse's uniform entered the room, introducing herself as McLean Nurse. [1683] \"My patient is ready to see you, Inspector Corrado. She's been looking forward to meeting you.\u201d\n[1684] \"I'll do my best not to upset her,\" Corrado promised. [1685] \"I should warn you about what to expect. You'll find Mrs. Goddard to be perfectly normal. She'll talk and enjoy talking, and then - suddenly - her energy will collapse. At that point, please leave immediately and call me. You'll see that she's mostly kept alive by morphine. Most of the time, she's in a half-conscious state. I've given her a shot of stimulant to stay awake for your visit. As the stimulant wears off, she'll return to her semi-comatose state.\" [1686] \"I understand, Nurse MacLane. Would it be appropriate for me to ask about Mrs. Goddard's exact health condition?\" [1687] \"Well, Mr. Corrado, she is a dying woman. Her life expectancy is only a few weeks. If you find it strange that she has not passed away years ago, you may be right, but that is the truth. What has kept Mrs. Goddard alive is her intense desire and love for life. For someone who has suffered for years and has not left her home in fifteen years, it may seem unusual, but it is true. Mrs. Goddard has never been a robust woman, yet her will to survive has been remarkable. She is still a very charming woman, you will find.\" [1688] Corrado was led into a large bedroom with a fire burning, where an elderly woman lay on a bed with a canopy. Although she was only a few years older than Letitia Blacklock, her frail body made her look much older. [1689] Her hair was neatly combed, and a light blue woolen wrap covered her neck and shoulders. The lines on her face expressed pain, but there was also sweetness. It was strange that her faded blue eyes sparkled with what Corrado could only describe as a mischievous gaze. [1690] \"Well, this is quite interesting,\" she said. \"I don't often have police visits. I hear that Letitia Blacklock was not seriously injured in that attack? How is my dear Blanche?\" [1691] \"She is fine, Mrs. Goddard. She sends her regards.\" [1692] \"I haven't seen her in a long time... Many years have passed since she returned to England, and I invited her to stay here, but she said that after so much time, meeting old acquaintances would be painful. Perhaps she was right... Blythe is a very wise woman. About a year ago, an old school friend of mine came to visit me, but, hmm!\" [1693] She smiled slightly. \"We looked at each other with disgust. After asking 'Do you remember?' there was nothing more to say. It was quite awkward.\" [1694] Mr. Coleridge willingly let her ramble on, hoping to gain insight into the supposed family atmosphere between the Goddards and Blacklock. [1695] \"I suppose,\" Beryl shrewdly asked, \"you want to know about the money? Randall left a will stating that the money should go to Blythe upon my death. Of course, Randall never thought I would outlive him. He was a big, strong man who never got sick; I was always complaining about aches and pains. Doctors came every few days, looking at my condition with a frown.\" [1696] \"I don't think 'complaining' is the right word, Mrs. Goddard.\" [1697] The old lady chuckled. [1698] \"I didn't mean complaining in the sense of whining. I never felt too sorry for myself. But being so weak, everyone naturally thought I would go first. But it wasn't that way. No, not at all.\" [1699] \"Exactly, why did your husband do that with his money?\" [1700] \"You mean why did he leave it to Blair? Not for the reasons you might imagine.\" [1701] Kroll looked at the frail old lady on the big bed with interest. He realized that Bella Goddard had always enjoyed and still enjoyed being a woman. She blinked at him.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[128] \"You're still like a little girl, Round Round.\" [129] Reverend Julian Harmon, at the age of sixty, was a fine specimen of nature's creation, as he looked twenty-five years younger than he actually was. [130] \"I know I'm silly.\" [131] \"You're not silly, Roundy, you're clever.\" [132] \"No, I'm not clever, I'm not at all smart, even though I try... I like it when you read to me, tell me history and other things. And in the evenings, reading Gibbon's works to me seems a bit foolish. Because some things in Gibbon's books make you want to fall asleep when it's cold outside and the fire is warm and cozy.\" [133] Julian laughed. [134] \"But I do like listening to you read, Julian. Tell me that old parson's story about Ahasuerus again.\" [135] \"You can recite it, Roundy.\" [136] \"Tell me again, please.\" [137] So her husband obliged and began telling the story. [138] \"This is an old pastor named Skrinker. One day, someone went to his church, and he was leaning on the pulpit, preaching to two old cleaning women. He wagged his finger at them and said, 'Ah-ha! I know what you're thinking. You're thinking that the first verse [4] of Ahasuerus the Great is Artaxerxes II. But he's not! He's Artaxerxes III.' He announced triumphantly.\" [139] Julian always thought the story wasn't particularly funny, but it always made Roundy laugh. [140] Her laughter rang out. [141] \"Poor old dear,\" she exclaimed. \"I think someday you'll be just like him, Julian.\" [142] Julian looked quite uncomfortable. [143] \"I know,\" he concurred humbly. \"I do feel that sometimes I can't find a simple and appropriate way.\" [144] \"I'm not worried,\" Roundy said, standing up and stacking the breakfast dishes onto a large tray. \"Mrs. Bartlett told me yesterday that Mr. Bartlett, who never used to go to church and was a local atheist, now goes every Sunday just to hear you preach.\" [145] She imitated Mrs. Bartlett's affected tone, continuing:\n[146] \"'And one day, madam, Mr. Bartlett said to Mr. Timkins from Worsdale, we at Chipping Clegg-horn are the real cultural ones. Unlike Mr. Gross from Worsdale, who speaks to the congregation as if they were all uncultured children. True culture, Mr. Bartlett said, is our advantage. Our clergyman is a highly educated gentleman. From Oxford, not Milchester, and he imparts all the benefits of his education to us. He knows all about Romans, Greeks, Babylonians, Assyrians, even the cat of the parsonage, Mr. Bartlett said, is named after a king of Assyria!' So, that's your glory.\" Roundabout concluded her speech triumphantly, \"Well, I must get back to work, or I'll never finish. Here, Tigris and Euphrates, have your herring bones.\" [147] She opened the door, propped it open with her foot, and hurried off with the tray of cutlery, singing in a loud but slightly off-key voice:\n[148] It's a fine day to kill time,\n[149] Like a warm May day,\n[150] With the town's police nowhere to be found. [151] The sound of clattering dishes being placed in the sink drowned out the next line. But as Julien Harmon left the house, he heard the final triumphant and daring line of the song:\n[152] Murder's on the scene today. [153] * * *\n[154] [1] A nickname for Wuthering Heights. [155] [2] Confirmation: a Christian ceremony in which a person who has been baptized is confirmed as a member of the church. According to Christian doctrine, a child must be baptized and confirmed before they can become a full member of the church. [156] Edward Gibbon (1737-1794), a British historian. [157] A section of Scripture read during the morning prayer service in the Church of England. [158] Chapter 2: Breakfast Mystery\n[159] 1\n[160] In the small courtyard, breakfast was also being served. [161] Miss Blacklock was the mistress of the house, over sixty years old, sitting at the head of the table. She wore a country-style tweed suit and a short necklace made of large imitation pearls, which looked a bit out of place with her outfit. She was reading the section of the Daily Mail about the events on Necot Street. Julie Simmons was browsing the Telegraph in a lackluster manner, Patrick Simmons was examining the crossword puzzle in the Times, and Miss Dora Bunner was immersed in the local weekly newspaper. [162] Miss Blacklock was chuckling to herself. Meanwhile, Patrick grumbled, \"It's 'sticking' instead of 'adhesive' [1] \u2013 I got caught on that word.\" [163] Suddenly, there was a loud \"cluck\" from Miss Bunner's side. [164] \"Lettie [2] \u2013 Lettie \u2013 did you see this? What does this mean?\" [165] \"What's the matter, Dora?\" [166] \"An unusual notice, clearly inviting everyone to the small park. But what does it mean?\" [167] \"Let me see, dear Miss Bonner -\"\n[168] Miss Bonner handed the newspaper to Miss Blacklock, who reached out and took it, then pointed to the notice with her index finger. [169] \"Look here, Lettie.\" [170] Miss Blacklock looked at it, raised her eyebrows, and then loudly read out the notice. [171] \"A murder will take place at Little Park on Friday, October 29th at 6:30 p.m. Please be informed. Friends are requested to attend, no further notice will be given.\" [172] \"Patrick, is this your doing?\" she asked sharply, her gaze falling on the young man at the other end of the table, whose handsome face was well-known to all. [173] Patrick Simmons immediately denied it. [174] \"No, it's not, Aunt Letty. How could you think that? Why should I know about it?\" [175] \"Because you're the kind of person who would do something like that,\u201d Miss Blacklock said acerbically, \"to pull a prank or something.\" [176] \"Prank? Not a chance.\" [177] \"You, Julia?\" [178] \"Of course not,\" Julia answered listlessly. [179] \"You think, Mrs. Hyde-Miller...\" Miss Bonner trailed off, glancing at the empty seat at the table. [180] \"I don't think our Philippa would attempt such a whimsical thing,\" Patrick interjected. \"She's a serious girl, really.\" [181] \"But what is the purpose?\" Julia yawned, \"What does it mean?\" [182] Miss Blacklock spoke slowly, \"I suppose... it's some kind of silly trick.\" [183] \"But why?\" Miss Bonner exclaimed, \"What's the point? It looks like a silly joke, and very poor taste.\" [184] Her slack cheeks trembled with anger, and her myopic eyes flashed with fury. [185] Miss Blacklock smiled at her. [186] \"Don't worry about it, Bonnie,\" she said. \"It's just some guy's idea of a joke, but I'd like to know who's behind it.\" [187] \"It says tonight,\" Miss Bunner pointed out, \"Six thirty tonight. What do you think will happen?\" [188] \"Death!\" Patrick said grimly, \"The Death of Delight.\" [189] Miss Bunner gave a little shriek. \"Shut up, Patrick,\" Miss Blacklock said. [190] \"I was only referring to the special cake that Micky makes,\" Patrick said apologetically. \"You know we always call it the Death of Delight.\" [191] Miss Blacklock smiled vaguely. [192] Miss Bunford persisted. \"But, Letty, do you really think\u2014\"\n[193] Her friend interrupted her with a reassuring and comforting tone. [194] \"As for what will happen at six thirty,\" she announced dryly, \"half the village will be here, all with their curiosity piqued. I'd better have some sherry on hand.\" [195] 2\n[196] \"You're worried, aren't you, Lotte?\" [197] Miss Blacklock was startled into looking up from her place at the writing table, where she had been idly sketching small fish on blotting paper. Now she raised her head to look into her friend's anxious face. [198] She didn't know what to say to Miss Bonner, for she knew that Miss Bonner could not bear any more anxiety or sorrow. Miss Blacklock fell silent, lost in thought. [199] She and Miss Bonner had been at school together. Miss Bonner had been a golden-haired beauty, not very bright, but her looks and vivacious personality had made her popular. She must have - Miss Blacklock reflected - married a decent officer or a country lawyer. She had so many redeeming qualities: kindness, devotion, loyalty, but life had been hard on Miss Bonner, forcing her to fend for herself. Despite her best efforts, she had never quite managed to make ends meet. [200] These two friends had not seen each other for some time. However, six months ago, Miss Blacklock had received a letter from Miss Bonner, disjointed in thought and full of sorrow. Miss Bonner's health was deteriorating, and she lived alone in a tiny cottage, subsisting on a pension. She tried to make ends meet by doing some needlework, but her fingers were stiff from rheumatism. In her letter, she talked about their school days - before life had forced them apart - but could her old friend offer her any help? [201] Miss Blacklock had impulsively written back. Poor Miss Bunner, poor, pretty, stupid, and shallow Miss Bunner. She had rushed to Miss Bunner's aid, bringing her back to settle in the country, and concocting excuses to comfort her: \"There are too many chores for me to handle alone, so I need someone to help me manage the household.\" However, before long - the doctor had warned her - she would often feel that bringing Miss Bunner was a mistake. Miss Bunner made a mess of everything, causing the eccentric foreign \"helper\" to become agitated; she would count the laundry wrong, misplace bills and letters, and sometimes irritate the capable Miss Blacklock to the point of anger and pain. However, poor and confused Miss Bunner was so loyal and eager to help, feeling so happy and proud to be able to do something for others - unfortunately, she was utterly unreliable. [202] Miss Blacklock spoke sharply. [203] \"Don't do that, Miss Bunner. You know I told you -\"\n[204] \"Oh,\" Miss Bunner said apologetically, \"I know. I forgot, but - but you're worried, aren't you?\" [205] \"Worried? No,\" she added sincerely, \"at least, not very. Are you talking about the silly advertisement in The Times?\" [206] \"Yes. Even if it's a joke, it seems - it seems cruel.\" [207] \"Cruel?\" [208] \"Yes. I just feel that there's something a bit cruel about it. I mean - it's not a nice joke.\" [209] Miss Blacklock looked at her friend, regarding the soft eyes, long and obstinate mouth, the slightly upturned nose. Poor Dora, so caught up in her own world, so muddled, so involved, so irritating. A lovable and over-sensitive old fool, but strangely intuitive. [210] \"I think you're right, Dora,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"It's not a nice joke.\" [211] \"I don't like it at all,\" Miss Dora Bonner said in a tone of unusual firmness, \"It frightens me.\" [212] \"Nonsense,\" Miss Blacklock retorted sharply. [213] \"It's dangerous, that's what I feel about it, just as if someone had put a bomb in a parcel and sent it to you.\" [214] \"Darling, it's just some silly fool trying to play a trick.\" [215] \"But it's not funny.\" [216] Indeed it was... Miss Blacklock's expression gave away her thoughts, and Miss Bunner triumphantly exclaimed: \"There, you think so yourself!\u201d\n[217] \"But, Miss Bunner, my dear...\u201d\n[218] She broke off. A young woman, her bosom swelling with emotion, her figure wrapped in a tight knit sweater, her lower half clad in a dazzlingly bright skirt, her oily dark hair coiled atop her head, dark eyes glowing, burst in at the door. [219] \"Can I speak to you? Can I, may I not, please?\u201d She fired off her questions like a machine gun. [220] Miss Blacklock sighed. [221] \"Of course, Mickey, what's the matter?\" [222] Sometimes she thought that it would be easier to do all the household chores and cooking herself rather than dealing with this refugee \"helper\" who was constantly moody. [223] \"I'm going to tell you - is that the right order? I'm going to notify you, I'm leaving - I'm leaving right now!\" [224] \"Why? Has someone disturbed you?\" [225] \"Yes, I'm very upset,\" Mickey said dramatically, \"I don't want to die! I've escaped from the continent of Europe. My family is dead - all killed - my mother, little brother, and dear little niece - all, all killed. But I escaped - I hid. I came to England. I work. I do things that I would never do in my own country - I -\"\n[226] \"I understand all that, Miss Blacklock,\" said the woman firmly. These words were always on Mickey's lips. \"But why do you want to leave now?\" [227] \"Because they're coming to kill me!\" [228] \"Who's coming to kill you?\" [229] \"My enemies. Nazis! Maybe this time it's the Bolsheviks. They found me here, they're coming to kill me. I saw it \u2013 yes \u2013 in the newspaper!\" [230] \"Oh, you mean in The News?\" [231] \"Here, it's right here.\" Midge took the newspaper she had been hiding behind and showed it to her. \"See\u2014it says there's a murder, right here in the park. That's here, isn't it? At six thirty tonight. Ah! I don't want to wait to be killed\u2014no, I don't!\" [232] \"But why should this be about you? This is\u2014we think it's a joke.\" [233] \"A joke? Murder isn't a joke, is it?\" [234] \"No, of course not. But, my dear child, if someone wanted to kill you, they wouldn't advertise it in the newspaper, would they?\" [235] \"You don't think they would?\" Midge seemed to shiver. \"You don't think, perhaps, they don't really intend to kill anybody? Maybe they're after you, Miss Blacklock.\" [236] \"I certainly don't believe anyone wants to harm me.\" Miss Blacklock replied lightly, \"And honestly, Miki, I can't see why anyone would want to harm you. After all, what reason would they have?\" [237] \"Because they're all bad... very, very bad people. I tell you, my mother, my little brother, my adorable niece\u2026\"\n[238] \"Yes, that's true,\" Miss Blacklock quickly cut her off, \"But I really can't believe anyone would want to harm you, Miki. Of course, if you want to leave, I can't stop you. But I think it would be unwise if you did.\" [239] As Miki hesitated, Miss Blacklock added decisively, \"Let's cook the beef that the butcher brought over for lunch. That meat looks tough.\" [240] \"I'll make you a stewed beef, a secret recipe.\" [241] \"If you want to call that dish that, of course. And perhaps you should use up that hard cheese to make some cheese straws. I think someone might come by tonight for a drink.\" [242] \"Tonight? What do you mean, tonight?\" [243] \"Six thirty.\" [244] \"But that's the time they said it was in the paper. Why come then? Why do they want to come?\" [245] \"They're coming for the funeral,\" Miss Blacklock's eyes sparkled, \"Well, that's all, Mickey. I'm busy. Go out and close the door when you leave.\" She said firmly. [246] \"She should be able to calm down for a while.\" The door closed with a suspicious look on Midge's face, she concluded. [247] \"You're really efficient, Letty,\" Miss Bonner said admiringly. [248] * *\n[249] The former is spelled \"adherent,\" and the latter is \"adhesive.\" [250] \"Lettie\" is a nickname for Letticia. [251] [3] A nickname for Bonner. [252] Chapter 3: Six O'Clock Half Past\n[253] 1\n[254] \"All right, everything is ready.\" Miss Blacklock said. She surveyed the drawing room with a critical eye: a rose-patterned tablecloth on the table against the wall, two bronze-colored chrysanthemums in vases, violets in small vases, and a silver cigarette case on the center table. The tray with drinks was on the table. [255] The small park is a house with an early Victorian style, of medium size, with a long shaded gallery and several green shutters. The narrow drawing room was intercepted by the roof of the gallery. At one end of the room, there were two doors leading to a small room with a bay window. The previous occupants had removed the two doors and replaced them with velvet drapes. Miss Blacklock removed the drapes, merging the two rooms into one. There were two fireplaces at each end of the drawing room, but neither was lit, yet the room was still warm. [256] \"You turned on the central heating?\" Patrick asked. [257] Miss Blacklock nodded. [258] \"It's been misty and damp lately, and the whole house feels damp. I had Evans turn it on before he left.\" [259] \"You used the very, very precious coal dust?\" Patrick asked sarcastically. [260] \"As you said, yes, precious coal dust, otherwise we would have had to use even more precious coal. You know, the fuel office doesn't even give us our weekly allowance unless we can prove we have no other means of cooking.\" [261] \"I suppose everyone had a big pile of coal back then?\" Julia asked with interest, as if hearing a fairy tale. [262] \"Yes, and it was cheap.\" [263] \"Anyone could buy it, and as much as they wanted, without filling out any forms, and there weren't any shortages, were there? Plenty of coal?\" [264] \"And it was of all kinds and qualities \u2013 unlike the coal now, which is all slag.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nMiss Blacklock is waiting for you.\" [547] Colardoke noticed that the hallway was long and seemed to have doors everywhere. [548] The young woman pushed open the door on the left and said, \"Colardoke Inspector has arrived, Aunt Letty. Mickey didn't want to open the door, she locked herself in her room and made some strange moaning sounds. I don't think we'll be having any lunch.\" [549] \"She doesn't like the police,\" she added in an explanatory tone to Colardoke. Then she went out and closed the door behind her. [550] Colardoke approached the hostess of the small estate. [551] He saw a tall, well-preserved woman of about sixty years. Her gray hair was naturally wavy, with an elegant hairstyle that highlighted her intelligent, determined face. Her gray eyes were piercing, and her jawline was square and resolute, with a bandage wrapped around her left ear. She was not wearing makeup, only a well-tailored tweed suit, skirt, and sweater. Over the sweater's collar, she wore an unexpected string of antique jade carvings \u2013 a subtle Victorian sentiment. [552] Standing beside her was a woman of similar age, with a round face and an anxious expression. Her hair was disheveled and escaping from a hairnet. Corradino had no difficulty recognizing her as \"Dora Bunner \u2013 companion,\" as mentioned in the report by Inspector Legge. Legge had added an unofficial comment about her: \"Inept!\" [553] Miss Blacklock spoke in a pleasant, well-bred tone. [554] \"Good morning, Detective Corradino. This is my friend Miss Bunner, who helps me with the household. Please sit down. You don't smoke, I presume?\" [555] \"I'm afraid not, Miss Blacklock, during duty hours.\" [556] \"How unfortunate!\" [557] Corraduccio quickly and carefully surveyed the room. A typical Victorian double living room. One had two long windows, and the other had a bay window... chairs... sofas... a table in the middle with a large vase of chrysanthemums - another on the windowsill - all fresh and pretty, but lacking novelty. The only thing that didn't fit the whole scene was a silver vase with wilted violets. It was placed on a table next to the archway leading to the inner room. It was hard to imagine Miss Blacklock tolerating dead flowers in her well-maintained house, and in Corraduccio's opinion, if anything out of the ordinary had happened in this house, this was the only clue. [558] He spoke up: \"I assume the accident happened in this room, Miss Blacklock?\" [559] \"Indeed.\" [560] \"You should have seen it last night,\" Miss Bonna exclaimed excitedly, \"it was a mess. Two small tables were knocked over, one of the legs of one broke - everyone bumping into each other - and someone dropped a lit cigarette, burning one of the best pieces of furniture. Those people - especially the young ones - don't care about these things... fortunately, no ceramics were broken...\". [561] Miss Blacklock interrupted her kindly but firmly. [562] \"Dora, all this may be annoying, but it's just trivial matters. I think it's best to answer Inspector Kolladok's questions.\" [563] \"Thank you, Miss Blacklock. I'll ask about what happened last night right away. First, I'd like to know when was the last time you saw the deceased - Rudi Scherz?\" [564] \"Rudi Scherz?\" Miss Blacklock looked slightly surprised. \"I vaguely remember... never mind, it's irrelevant. The first time I met him was when I went to the Medenham Pleasure Gardens to buy something, about three weeks ago. We - Miss Bonner and I - had lunch at the Royal Spa Hotel. After lunch, we were about to leave when I heard someone call my name, it was this young man. He said, 'Are you Miss Blacklock, isn't it?' Then he said I probably didn't remember him, he was the son of the owner of the Alpine Hotel in Monte Carlo, where my sister and I stayed for almost a year during the war.\" [565] \"The Alpine Hotel in Monte Carlo,\" Kolladok repeated, \"Do you remember him, Miss Blacklock?\" [566] \"No, I don't. In fact, I can't recall ever seeing him before. The front desk clerks at hotels all look alike. My sister and I had a wonderful time in Monte Carlo, and the hotel owner was very helpful, so I was as polite as I could be to him and wished him well in England. He said that his father had sent him here for six months to learn hotel management. It all seemed perfectly natural.\" [567] \"What about the next encounter?\" [568] \"About ten days ago, he suddenly appeared here. I was very surprised to see him. He apologized for disturbing me and said that I was the only person he knew in England. He told me that his mother was seriously ill and that he urgently needed money to return to Switzerland.\" [569] \"But Lady Letitia didn't give him any money,\" Miss Bonner interjected, breathlessly. [570] \"That was a completely unbelievable story,\" said Miss Blacklock firmly. \"I knew he was a bad character, and that story about needing money to return to Switzerland was just a load of rubbish. His father could easily have sent a telegram to arrange for it to be taken care of here in England. Hotel owners always help each other out. At the time, I suspected that he had embezzled money or something like that. I paused, then continued in a dry tone, \"If you think I'm a heartless person, I should tell you that I've been a secretary for a prominent financier for many years, so I'm very cautious about people asking for money. I'm well aware of these so-called unfortunate stories.\" [571] \"The only thing that surprised me,\" she added thoughtfully, \"was how easily he gave up. He didn't come up with any other reasons and left immediately, as if he hadn't even expected to get any money from me.\" [572] \"Do you think now that he came here to scout out the situation, but made up an excuse?\" [573] Miss Blacklock nodded vigorously. [574] \"That's what I think now. I saw him out and he said something about the house. He said, 'Your dining room is very nice.' But of course it wasn't \u2014 it was a dark and shabby little room, and he was just looking for an excuse to take a look inside. Then he rushed past me and pulled the latch on the front door, saying, 'Let me.' Now, when I think back on it, he was trying to see the latch. In fact, like other people in the neighborhood, we don't lock our doors until dark, and anyone can come in.\" [575] \"And what about the side door? I understand there's a side door that leads to the garden?\" [576] \"Yes. I went out through that door just before the guests arrived to shut the ducks in.\" [577] \"Did you lock the door when you went out?\" [578] Miss Blacklock frowned. [579] \"I can't remember... I think I did. I'm sure I locked it when I came in.\" [580] \"It was just after a quarter to six then?\" [581] \"Something like that.\" [582] \"What about the front door?\" [583] \"It's usually locked later.\" [584] \"So Sherris could have easily come in through there, or he could have slipped in while you were shutting the ducks. He'd have had a good look at the layout, and he'd have noticed the hiding places - cupboards, that sort of thing. Yes, it all seems clear enough.\" [585] \"I beg your pardon, but there's still something I don't understand.\" Miss Blacklock said. \"Why on earth would anyone go to all this trouble to stage such a silly robbery?\" [586] \"You keep a lot of money at home, Miss Blacklock?\" [587] \"About five pounds in that drawer, and a couple of pounds in my purse.\" [588] \"What about jewelry?\" [589] \"One or two rings and a brooch, and the carved jade I'm wearing. You must agree with me, Inspector, the whole thing is absurd.\" [590] \"This was no ordinary break-in and robbery,\" Miss Bonner cried. \"I've been telling you so all along, Letty. This is revenge! Because you didn't give him the money! He deliberately shot at you \u2013 twice!\" [591] \"Ah,\" said Colonel Corcoran, \"let's talk about last night. What exactly happened, Miss Blacklock? Please tell me in your own words, as best you can remember.\" [592] Miss Blacklock paused for a moment. [593] \"The clock struck,\" she said. \"The one on the mantelpiece. I remember saying that if anything was going to happen, it would start right now. And then the clock struck. We all stood there in silence. It struck six-thirty, and suddenly, all the lights went out.\" [594] \"Which lights were on?\" [595] \"The lamps here and in the sitting room. The standard lamp and the two reading lamps weren't lit.\" [596] \"Did you see the flashlight first or hear a sound?\" [597] \"I'm not quite sure.\" [598] \"I'm certain it was the flashlight,\" said Dora Bunner, \"and then there was a creak. It was close.\" [599] \"And then, Miss Blacklock?\" [600] \"The door opened\u2014\"\n[601] \"Which one? There are two doors in this room.\" [602] \"Oh, this one. The other door won't open, it's just for show. The door opened, and out he came\u2014a man with a revolver in his left hand and a mask on his head. I was absolutely flabbergasted, of course, I thought it was just a silly joke. He said something\u2014I forget\u2014\"\n[603] \" 'Raise your hands, or I'll shoot!' \" Miss Bonner said dramatically. [604] \"Something like that,\" Miss Blacklock said doubtfully. [605] \"And then you both raised your hands?\" [606] \"Ah, yes,\" Miss Bonner said. \"We all raised our hands. I mean, it was part of the game.\" [607] \"I didn't,\" Miss Blacklock said firmly. \"It seemed ridiculous at the time. And I was quite annoyed with the whole thing.\" [608] \"Then what happened?\" [609] \"A flashlight beam shone right into my eyes, making me dizzy. Then, unbelievably, I heard a bullet whizz past my ear and hit the wall behind me. Someone screamed, and then I felt a searing pain in my ear and heard a second gunshot.\" [610] \"That was terrifying,\" Miss Bonner interjected. [611] \"What happened next, Miss Blacklock?\" [612] \"I'm not quite sure\u2014I was staggering around in pain and shock. The shadow turned around, stumbled, and then there was another gunshot. His flashlight went out, and everyone started pushing and shouting, all bumping into each other.\" [613] \"Where were you standing at the time, Miss Blacklock?\" [614] \"She was at the table. She was still holding the violet bottle.\" Miss Bonner panted. [615] \"I was right here,\" said Miss Blacklock, walking over to the small table by the arcade, \"still holding the cigarette box.\" [616] Inspector Corridor looked at the wall behind her, two bullet holes were clearly visible. The bullets had been removed and sent for comparison with the revolver. [617] \"You came close to losing your life, Miss Blacklock.\" He said calmly. [618] \"He was aiming at her,\" Miss Bonner said, \"He was after her! I saw him. He was flashing the flashlight around at everyone until he found her, and then he aimed and fired. He wanted to kill you, Letty.\" [619] \"Oh, Dorothy, dear, you're getting carried away again.\" [620] \"He was shooting at you,\" Dora insisted stubbornly, \"He wanted to kill you, but he missed, so he shot himself. That's what happened, I'm sure of it!\" [621] \"I don't believe for a moment that he intended to shoot himself,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"He's not the kind of man who would commit suicide.\" [622] \"Miss Blacklock, you thought all this was a joke until the moment the gun went off?\" [623] \"Of course, what else could I think?\" [624] \"You thought Patrick was behind it?\" [625] \"You started thinking it was Patrick,\" Miss Bonner reminded her. [626] \"Patrick?\" asked the inspector sharply. [627] \"Patrick Simmons,\" said Miss Blacklock, her voice rising in anger. \"I did wonder when I saw the ad, whether it might be his idea of a joke, but he denied it outright.\" [628] \"But you were worried, Letty,\" said Miss Bonner. \"You were worried, even though you tried to pretend you weren't. And you were right to be worried. The newspaper said the murder ad \u2013 it was actually a declaration of \u2013 a declaration of murder against you! If that man hadn't missed, you would have been killed. So what do we do now?\" [629] Dora was trembling all over. She looked as if she was about to burst into tears. [630] Miss Blacklock patted her on the shoulder. [631] \"It's all right, Dora dear - don't get excited, it's bad for you. Everything's fine. We've had some bad experiences, but they're over. Just for my sake, Dora, you must pull yourself together. You know, I depend on you to keep this house going.\" [632] \"Oh, my goodness, Leticia, thank you for reminding me! I wondered if they would return the missing pillowcase. I must make a note of it in my book. I'll take care of it right now.\" [633] \"Take these violets away, too,\" said Miss Blacklock. \"I hate dead flowers.\" [634] \"It's a shame. I just picked them yesterday. They didn't last long - oh, dear, I must have forgotten to add water to the vase. How could I forget? Now I must go and see about the laundry people. They could arrive any minute.\" [635] She brightened up and hurried out, looking relieved. [636] \"Her will isn't very strong,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"Excitement is bad for her. Is there anything else you need to know, Inspector?\" [637] \"I just want to know exactly how many people are in your house and something about them.\" [638] \"Sure, besides me and Dora, there are now two young cousins living here, Patrick and Julia Simmons.\" [639] \"Cousins? Not nephews and nieces?\" [640] \"No. Although they call me Aunt, they are actually distant cousins. Their mother is my second cousin.\" [641] \"They've been living here all the time?\" [642] \"Oh, no, only for the last two months. They were living in the south of France before the war. Patrick joined the navy, and I believe Julia was working in a government department. She was in Land's End. After the war, her mother wrote to me asking if they could come here as paying guests\u2014Julia was training as a pharmacist at Milchester General Hospital, and Patrick was studying for an engineering degree at Milchester University. Milchester, you know, is only fifty miles by bus, so I was glad to have them. The house is too big for me. They pay a small sum for board and lodging, and everything has gone well. She smiled and added, \"I like having young people around.\" [643] \"And then there's Mrs. Hyems, I believe?\" [644] \"Yes. She works as a helper in the garden at Deyce House\u2014that's Lucas's house\u2014but the little cottage there is occupied by an old couple, so Lucas asked me if I could find her a place to stay. She's a very nice girl, and her husband was killed in Italy. She has an eight-year-old boy who is at a preparatory school, and I arrange for him to come here during the holidays.\" [645] \"Does she help with the household chores?\" [646] \"She's a temporary gardener who comes on Tuesdays and Fridays. Mrs. Higgins from the village comes for five mornings a week. There's also a foreign refugee with a difficult-to-pronounce name working for me as a cook and so on. I'm afraid you'll find Miss Picker quite difficult to get along with; she has a persecution complex.\" [647] Corradino nodded. The thought of Inspector Legge's other offhand remark came to his mind. After rating Dora Bunner as \"subnormal\" and Letitia Blacklock as \"normal,\" the man had added the word \"fraud\" to describe Miss Picker. [648] Seeming to read his mind, Miss Blacklock said, \"Please don't be too prejudiced against her because she tells lies. I do believe that, like many frauds, there is some truth behind her lies. What I mean is, although her stories of violence become more and more outrageous, so that every unpleasant report in the papers seems to have happened to her family, she has indeed been very upset and has at least seen a relative killed. I believe that many people who have been uprooted feel \u2013 perhaps it's only natural \u2013 that they have a right to our attention and sympathy. They have suffered violence, so they exaggerate and invent. [649] \"But frankly, Miss Picker is a bit crazy. \" She added, \"She makes us all angry, she's suspicious, she's always scowling, she's always 'on edge,' and she thinks she's been insulted. However, I still feel sorry for her.\" [650] \"I'll do my best not to upset her,\" Corradino replied smoothly. \"Is it Miss Julia Simmons who let me in?\" [651] \"Yes. Patrick is out. You'll find Philippa Hammers working at Darcy House.\" [652] \"Thank you, Miss Blacklock. If possible, I'd like to see Miss Simmons.\" [653] Chapter 6: Three Testimonies\n[654] 1\n[655] Julia entered the room and sat down in the chair where Miss Blacklock had just been sitting. Her composure throughout the process somehow irritated Corradino. She looked at him calmly, waiting for him to ask questions. [656] Miss Blacklock left the room, leaving Julia alone with Colonel Caulder. [657] \"Please tell me about last night, Miss Simmons.\" [658] \"Last night?\" Julia appeared startled and murmured, \"Oh, we all slept very well. I suppose it was the reaction to the excitement.\" [659] \"I mean from six o'clock last night.\" [660] \"Ah, I see. Well, there were quite a few dull people \u2013\"\n[661] \"Who were they?\" [662] She gave him a placid glance. \"You know all about it, don't you?\" [663] \"I'm asking you, Miss Simmons,\" Colonel Corcoran said pleasantly. [664] \"I'm sorry. I always find conversation so tedious.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nAnd Mr. Edmund Swetnam, who disliked the Colonel, spoke up: \"The fact is, we're all under suspicion.\" [1824] \"But what's there to suspect?\" Mrs. Eastbrook asked again. [1825] \"Never mind, kitty,\" her husband said. [1826] \"On a pretext,\" Edmund said, \"the purpose is to catch the murderer on the spot.\" [1827] \"Oh, no, please don't say that, Mr. Sweetnam,\" Dora Bunner cried, \"I don't believe anyone here would want to kill dear, dear Letty.\" [1828] There was an awkward silence. Edmund's face turned red, and he whispered, \"It was just a joke.\" Philippa raised her voice and suggested going to listen to the six o'clock news, and everyone agreed eagerly. [1829] Patrick whispered to Julia, \"We need Mrs. Harmon here. She would certainly yell, 'But I think someone is still looking for an opportunity to kill Miss Blacklock!'\" [1830] \"I'm glad she and that Miss Maple didn't come,\" Julia said, \"That old woman is the type who likes to pry everywhere. I think her mind is full of all sorts of strange ideas. Very Victorian, I would say.\" [1831] While listening to the news, the conversation quickly turned to the horrors of nuclear war. Colonel Eastbrook claimed that the real threat to civilization was undoubtedly the Russians, while Edmund said he had some charming Russian friends - everyone reacted coldly to his statement. [1832] The guests thanked the hostess again, and the party came to an end. [1833] \"Did you have a good time, Bonnie?\" Miss Blacklock asked after the last guest had left. [1834] \"Yes, I did. But my head is aching. I think it's from excitement.\" [1835] \"It's the cake,\" Patrick said, \"I feel a bit unwell. And you've been nibbling on chocolate all morning.\" [1836] \"I think I'll lie down,\" Miss Bunns said. \"I'll take two aspirin, and then try to get a good night's sleep.\" [1837] \"That's a good idea,\" Miss Blacklock said. [1838] Miss Bunns went upstairs. [1839] \"Shall I put the ducks away for you, Aunt Letty?\" [1840] Miss Blacklock looked at Patrick sternly. [1841] \"If you promise to lock the door.\" [1842] \"I will. I swear I will.\" [1843] \"Have a sherry, Aunt Letty,\" said Julia. \"As my old nurse used to say, 'It will settle your stomach.' It's rather an unpalatable suggestion, but it's uncannily appropriate at the moment.\" [1844] \"Well, I dare say it may be a good thing. People are not used to greasy things nowadays. Ah, Bonnie, you did startle me. What's the matter?\" [1845] \"I can't find my aspirin,\" said Miss Bonner, looking disconsolate. [1846] \"Then take some of mine, on my bedside table.\" [1847] \"I have one on my dressing table too,\" said Felicia. [1848] \"Thank you \u2013 very much. If I can't find it \u2013 I remember where I put it, a new bottle. Where on earth did I put it?\" [1849] \"There's a pile of them in the bedroom,\" Julia said impatiently. \"There's plenty of aspirin in the house.\" [1850] \"I'm so careless with my things, it's so frustrating,\" Miss Bonner said, and went back upstairs. [1851] \"Poor old Bonnie,\" Julia adjusted her glasses, \"do you think we ought to give her sherry?\" [1852] \"No, I don't think so,\" said Miss Blacklock, \"she's too excited today, and it would do her no good. I'm afraid she'll feel worse tomorrow. But I do think she's had a nice time today.\" [1853] \"She's had a lovely time,\" said Phyllis. [1854] \"Let's give Midge a glass of sherry,\" Julia suggested, and when she heard Patrick come in she called, \"Pat, ask Midge to come here.\" [1855] Midge came in and Julia poured her a glass of sherry. [1856] \"Cheers to the best chef in the world,\" said Patrick. [1857] Micki felt satisfied \u2013 but felt she should protest. [1858] \"It's not like that. I'm actually not a chef. In my country, I do brain work.\" [1859] \"That's a waste,\" said Patrick, \"How can brain work compare to a master chef who cooks up delicious meals?\" [1860] \"Oh \u2013 I told you I didn't like \u2013\"\n[1861] \"I don't care what you like, my dear,\" Patrick said, \"I named it that. Let's toast to delicious death, and screw the aftertaste!\" [1862] 3\n[1863] \"Philippa, my dear, I want to talk to you.\" [1864] \"Oh, Miss Blacklock?\" [1865] Philippa raised her head slightly in surprise. [1866] \"What's bothering you, right?\" [1867] \"Bothering?\" [1868] \"I've noticed you've been looking worried lately. Anything wrong?\" [1869] \"Oh, no, Miss Blacklock. Why must there be something wrong?\" [1870] \"Hmm\u2014I was wondering\u2026I suppose maybe you and Patrick\u2014\"\n[1871] \"Patrick?\" Flickering with surprise. [1872] \"So it's not that, then. If I'm wrong, please forgive me. But you two are always together. Even though Patrick is my cousin, I don't think he would make a satisfactory husband. At least, not for a while.\" [1873] Flickering's face was stiff with no expression. [1874] \"I won't marry again.\" she said. [1875] \"Ah, not yet, my dear child, you're still young. But we don't need to discuss this. Is there any other trouble? You're not worried about\u2014like, money, for example?\" [1876] \"No, I'm fine.\" [1877] \"I know you sometimes worry about the children's education, so I wanted to tell you something. This afternoon, I drove to Milchester to see my lawyer, Mr. Beddingfield