t you?\" (But, anyway, you did quite well, Philippa. You had the upper hand, didn't you?) [1900] \"Oh, Julia\u2014I didn't mean to\u2014I never intended to\u2014\" (Oh, Julia\u2014I didn't mean to\u2014I never intended to\u2014)\n[1901] \"Didn't you? You certainly did. You're dissatisfied with everything, aren't you? Starving for money. But remember this\u2014if anyone offs Aunt Letty, you're the prime suspect.\" [1902] \"But I wouldn't. If\u2014if I had her killed before she could wait, that would be stupid\u2014\"\n[1903] \"So you know that old bat in Scotland is on her deathbed? I was wondering\u2026 Philly, now I'm starting to believe you're quite a formidable opponent.\" [1904] \"I don't want to interfere with you and Patrick.\" [1905] \"Don't you, my dear? I'm sorry\u2014but I don't believe you.\" [1906] Chapter 16: The Inspector Returns\n[1907] Inspector Corrado takes the night train back, but he sleeps poorly. He keeps dreaming, dreams that are more like nightmares. Over and over again, he runs through the dim corridors of a castle, trying to get somewhere, or to stop something. Finally, he dreams that he wakes up, a great sense of relief washing over him. Then, his compartment door slides open, and Letitia Blacklock sticks her bloodied head in, looking at him reproachfully and saying, \"Why didn't you save me? You could have done it if you'd tried.\" [1908] This time, he really wakes up. [1909] Thankfully, Inspector Corrado finally arrives in Milchester. He goes straight to the station and reports to Ridestead, who listens attentively. [1910] \"This trip didn't advance the case,\" he says, \"but it did confirm what Miss Blacklock told you. Pip and Emma\u2014well, let me think.\" [1911] \"Patrick and Julia Simmons' ages match up, Inspector. Assuming we can confirm that Miss Blacklock never saw them as adults \u2013\u201d\n[1912] Reddlesdale smiles slightly and says, \"Our ally Miss Marple has already confirmed that for us. In fact, Miss Blacklock hadn't seen them until two months ago.\" [1913] \"Well, then, it seems clear, Inspector \u2013\u201d\n[1914] \"It's not that simple, Corradino. We've been checking, and based on the information we have, Patrick and Julia seem to have no connection to the case. Patrick's naval records are genuine \u2013 he's performed well, with no signs of disobeying orders. We've also checked with Cannes, and a woman who claims to be Mrs. Simmons angrily says her son and daughter are, of course, living with her cousin Letitia Blacklock in Chipping Cleghorn.\" [1915] \"But is that Mrs. Simmons really the true Mrs. Simmons?\" [1916] \"She's been called Mrs. Simmons for a long time, I can only say that,\" Ridestone replied dryly. [1917] \"That seems clear enough. It's just\u2014the two match. The ages match, and Miss Blacklock herself doesn't recognize them. If you want to find Pip and Emma, there they are.\" [1918] The commissioner nodded thoughtfully and pushed a sheet of paper towards Corradino. [1919] \"This is some of the information we've gathered on Mrs. Eastbrook.\" [1920] The inspector raised his eyebrows as he read. [1921] \"Very interesting,\" he said. \"She kept the old man in the dark about it, didn't she? But I don't see how it's relevant to the case.\" [1922] \"On the surface, it doesn't seem to be.\" [1923] \"But this one does pertain to Mrs. Hyde.\" [1924] Corrigan raised his eyebrows again. [1925] \"I think I'll have to talk to this lady again,\" he said. \"Do you think this information could be relevant to the case?\" [1926] \"I think it might be. Of course, it could also be a waste of time\u2026\"\n[1927] The two fell silent for a moment. [1928] \"Any progress from Fletcher, Chief?\" [1929] \"Fletcher is very active. After obtaining Miss Blacklock's consent, he conducted a routine search of the mansion, but found nothing significant. Then he tried to find out who had the opportunity to oil the door and who was in the mansion when the foreign girl was out. The situation is more complicated than we thought, as she seems to go out for a walk in the afternoon. Usually to the village, to have a cup of coffee at 'The Bluebird.' So, when Miss Blacklock and Miss Bonner go out \u2013 which is usually in the afternoon \u2013 to pick blackberries, it's open for anyone to come in.\" [1930] \"And the door was never locked, then?\" [1931] \"It used to be. But I imagine it would be locked now.\" [1932] \"What results did Fletcher get? Who went into the house when it was empty?\" [1933] \"Actually, they all did.\" [1934] Ridestad looked at the page in front of him. [1935] \"Miss Mulgrew took a hen to hatch eggs. This sounds a bit redundant, but that's what she said. She was very flustered and contradictory in her statements. But Fletcher believes it was due to her personality rather than guilt.\" [1936] \"Maybe so,\" Corradino admitted. \"She was flustered.\" [1937] \"Next was Mrs. Sweetnam, who came to pick up the beef that Miss Blacklock had left on the kitchen table for her. Because Miss Blacklock drove to Milchester that day, and every time she went there, she always brought her some beef. Does that mean anything to you?\" [1938] Corradino pondered. [1939] \"Why didn't Miss Blacklock leave the beef at Mrs. Sweetnam's house on her way back from Milchester?\" [1940] \"I don't know, but she didn't. Mrs. Sweetnam said that Miss Blacklock always left the beef on the kitchen table, and she, Mrs. Sweetnam, liked to wait until Miss Blacklock wasn't around to take it, because Miss Blacklock could be quite rude sometimes.\" [1941] \"That does make sense. Who's next?\" [1942] \"Miss Inchcliff. She said she hadn't been there recently, but she actually had. Because Mickey saw her come out of the side door, and Mrs. Barton too - she's a local. Miss Inchcliff later admitted that she might have gone, but she forgot, didn't remember what she was doing, and said she probably just dropped by.\" [1943] \"That is quite strange.\" [1944] \"Obviously just like her behavior. Then there's Mrs. Eastbrook, who walks her dog on that path and stopped by to see if Blacklock had a knitting pattern she could borrow, but Blacklock wasn't there. She said she waited inside for a while.\" [1945] \"So that's what happened. Perhaps she was snooping around, or just oiling the door. And what about the Colonel?\" [1946] \"One day he went over with a book about India, which Miss Blacklock had expressed an interest in.\" [1947] \"She really did?\" [1948] \"She said she'd rather not look at it, but it didn't do any good.\" [1949] \"That's a fair point,\" said Cora Dedalus. \"If someone keeps pestering you to borrow something, you can't get rid of it!\" [1950] \"We don't know if Edmund Swetnam has been there. He was vague about it, saying he sometimes pops in on his way to do something for his mother, but he doesn't think it's been recently.\" [1951] \"After all, it's still too early to draw conclusions.\" [1952] \"Yes.\" [1953] \"Ridderdale smiled slightly and said, \"Miss Maple was very active. Fletcher reported that she went to 'Bluebird' House for coffee one morning, then to the Gravel Pits for sherry, and to the Little Paddocks for tea. She admired Mrs. Swetnam's garden and paid a visit to Colonel Eastbrook's house to admire his Indian antiques.\" [1954] \"She might be able to tell us if this Colonel Eastbrook is a real or a fake.\" [1955] \"She will find out, I agree - he seems to be all right. We will check with the British authorities in the Far East to confirm his identity.\" [1956] \"Meanwhile,\" Corrado interrupted him, \"do you think Miss Blacklock would agree to leave?\" [1957] \"Leave Chipping Cleghorn?\" [1958] \"Yes. Perhaps with the faithful Bunner in tow, to a place where no one knows her. Why doesn't she go to Scotland to stay with Bella Goddard? It's a remote place.\" [1959] \"Just stay there and wait for her to die? I don't think she would do that. I don't think any kind-hearted woman would like that suggestion.\" [1960] \"If it's about saving her life -\"\n[1961] \"Well, Kollarik, killing someone isn't as easy as you think.\u201d\n[1962] \"Isn't it, sir?\u201d\n[1963] \"Well\u2014in one sense, I agree, it's quite simple. There are plenty of ways of doing it, like using weed killer, or hitting her on the head when she comes out to let the chickens out, or hiding behind the hedge and throwing a pot at her. That's all quite simple. But to kill someone and not be suspected of it, that's not so easy. The murderer must realize he's being watched. The carefully planned scheme has failed, and our unknown murderer has to make other arrangements.\u201d\n[1964] \"I understand that, sir. But the murderer has to consider time. Mrs. Gowder is dying, and she might die any minute. That means the murderer can't wait.\u201d\n[1965] \"Quite.\u201d\n[1966] \"And another thing, Chief, the killer must know that we are investigating everyone.\" [1967] \"And that takes time,\" Laidesdale sighed. \"That means checking with the East, in India. Yes, it's a tedious and time-consuming job.\" [1968] \"So, that's another reason to hurry.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[265] \"That must have been a wonderful world,\" Julia said with a reverential tone. [266] Miss Blacklock smiled. \"Looking back, I do think so. But, to be fair, I am getting old and it's natural to prefer my own era. But you young people shouldn't think that way.\" [267] \"If I were in the past, I might just stay at home, tending to flowers and writing notes... but why did people in the past write notes? Who did they write to?\" [268] \"They wrote to the people you would call now, Julia,\" Miss Blacklock's eyes sparkled, \"I doubt you even know how to write, do you?\" [269] \"Not like the way I discovered that interesting 'Guide to Correspondence' book that taught you how to politely refuse a widower's proposal.\" [270] \"I doubt you would enjoy staying at home as much as you think,\" Miss Blacklock said, \"Women in the past had family responsibilities, you know.\u201d Her voice became flat, \"But I know little about that. Myself and Bonnie,\u201d she smiled affectionately at Dora Bunner, \"went out into the world very early.\" [271] \"Yes, that's right,\" Miss Bunner agreed. \"I can't forget those mischievous children. Of course, Letty was smart, she was a highly educated woman, a secretary to a prominent financier.\" [272] The door opened, and Felicia Hyms walked in. She was tall and attractive, but her face looked tired. She looked around the room in surprise. [273] \"Hello,\" she said. \"Is there a party? No one told me.\" [274] \"Of course,\" Patrick exclaimed. \"Our Felicia doesn't know. I'll bet she's the only one in Chipping Cleghorn who doesn't know.\" [275] Felicia looked at him quizzically. [276] \"Look, everyone,\" Patrick waved his hand dramatically. \"A crime scene!\" [277] Philippa Hammers looked thoroughly confused. [278] \"Here,\" Patrick pointed to the two large bowls of chrysanthemums. \"These are wreaths, and these trays of cheese straws and olives are for the funeral feast.\" [279] Philippa looked at Miss Blacklock with confusion. [280] \"Is this a joke?\" she asked. \"I'm always slow to understand jokes.\" [281] \"This is a very poor joke,\" Dora Bunner emphasized, \"I don't like it at all.\" [282] \"Show her the notice,\" Miss Blacklock instructed. \"I must go and shut up the ducks. It's getting dark, and people will be arriving soon.\" [283] \"I'll do it,\" Philippa said. [284] \"No, dear, you've been busy all day.\" [285] \"I'll do it, Aunt Letty,\" Patrick volunteered. [286] \"No, you're not going,\" Miss Blacklock said firmly, \"last time you didn't even latch the door properly.\" [287] \"I'll go, Aunt Letty, dear,\" Miss Bonner called, \"really, I'd be glad to. I'll just put on my high boots \u2013 where did I put my woolen vest?\" [288] But at this point, Miss Blacklock had already smiled and left the room. [289] \"Never mind, Bonnie,\" Patrick said. \"Aunt Letty is so efficient, she won't tolerate anyone doing things for her. She really likes to do everything herself.\" [290] \"She does like that,\" Julia said. [291] \"I've never seen you offer to help with anything,\" her brother said. [292] Julia smiled lazily. [293] \"You said yourself, Letty Aunt likes to do things herself,\" she pointed out, \"and besides,\" she extended a slender leg clad in sheer stockings, \"I'm wearing my best socks.\" [294] \"Dying in silk stockings!\" Patrick exclaimed dramatically. [295] \"Not silk\u2014nylon, you idiot.\" [296] \"Nylon doesn't sound classy enough,\" he said. [297] \"No one could do us a favor,\" Philippa complained loudly, \"tell me why everyone is so obsessed with death?\" [298] For a moment, everyone wanted to point out the newspaper to her, but couldn't find it as Mickey had taken it into the kitchen. [299] A few minutes later, Miss Blacklock returned. [300] \"All set,\" she said cheerfully, glancing at the clock, \"6:20. Someone will be here soon - unless I'm completely mistaken about my neighbors.\" [301] \"I can't see why anyone would come,\" Philippa looked perplexed. [302] \"Can't you, dear?... I dare say you can't. But most people are much better than you at it.\" [303] \"Philippa's attitude towards life is indifferent and uncaring,\" Julia commented sharply. [304] Philippa made no response to this. [305] Miss Blacklock looked around the living room. Midge had placed sherry and three plates on the table in the center of the room, with olives, cheese straws, and some odd little treats. [306] \"Patrick, if you don't mind, would you bring the tray - and the table, if you like - from the corner to the bay window in the other room? After all, I'm not having a party! I haven't invited anyone, and I don't want people to think I'm expecting them to turn up.\" [307] \"Are you trying to hide your foresight, Aunt Letty?\" [308] \"You're right, Patrick. Thank you, my dear child.\" [309] \"Now we can all pretend to be having a quiet evening at home,\" said Julia, \"and then be taken by surprise when people arrive.\" [310] Miss Blacklock picked up the bottle of sherry and stood there, hesitatingly holding it. [311] \"There's more than half a bottle, so it should be enough,\" Patrick reassured her. [312] \"Ah, yes, that's right\u2026\" she faltered, her face flushing slightly. [313] \"Patrick, could you please\u2026 there's an unopened bottle in the cabinet in the kitchen. Bring it over with the opener. We\u2014we should drink something new. This one has been open for a while.\" [314] Patrick went off on his mission without a word, returning with the new bottle and opener. As he placed the bottle in the tray, he curiously looked up at Miss Blacklock. [315] \"You seem quite serious about this, dear?\" he whispered. [316] \"Oh, my goodness!\" Miss Blacklock exclaimed in surprise. \"Truly, Letty, you're not thinking of\u2026\"\n[317] \"Shh,\" Miss Blacklock said quickly. \"The doorbell rang. You see, my intuition was right.\" [318] 2\n[319] Midge opened the living room door and let in Colonel Eastbrook and his wife. Her way of announcing the guests was unorthodox. [320] \"Colonel Eastbrook and his wife are here to see you.\" She casually announced. [321] Colonel Eastbrooke was a jovial and easygoing man who brushed off the slight embarrassment with ease. [322] \"We just happened to be passing by and thought we'd drop in,\" he said, and Julia couldn't help but chuckle. \"The night is so lovely, isn't it? I noticed you have the central heating on, ours is still off.\" [323] \"Your flowers are just beautiful,\" Colonel Eastbrooke's wife said, flattering them, \"so charming!\" [324] \"They're just some dried twigs and leaves, really,\" Julia replied. [325] Colonel Eastbrooke's wife greeted Phyllida Hemsley with an overly friendly air, subtly indicating that she knew Phyllida was not a farmhand. [326] \"How's Mrs. Lucas' garden doing?\" she asked, \"Do you think it can be revived? It was completely neglected during the war\u2014and then only one gardener was hired, that awful old man Ash who did nothing but sweep up leaves and plant cabbages.\" [327] \"It's still worth the effort,\" Phyllida answered, \"just needs a bit of time.\" [328] Micky opened the door and announced, \"The ladies from the Gravel Pits are here.\" [329] \"Good evening,\" Miss Inchclyde strode over, grabbed Miss Blacklock's hand and shook it firmly, \"I told Muggleton, 'Let's pay a visit to the Gravel Pits!' I wanted to ask you about your ducks' eggs.\" [330] \"It's getting so dark so early, isn't it?\" Miss Muggleton said nervously to Patrick, \"These chrysanthemums are just beautiful!\" [331] \"All leaves and no flowers,\" Julia said. [332] \"Why can't you just do as you're told?\" Patrick whispered to her. [333] \"You're running the central heating, aren't you?\" Miss Ingham said reproachfully. \"It's too early.\" [334] \"It always gets so damp in this house at this time of year,\" Miss Blacklock replied. [335] Patrick raised his eyebrows, silently indicating with a gesture to offer sherry, but Miss Blacklock replied with a signal: \"Too early.\" [336] She addressed Colonel Eastbrooke:\n[337] \"Did you import bulbs from Holland this year?\" [338] The door opened again, and Mrs. Swetnam came in with Edmund, looking sheepish, followed by a glum-faced Edmund. [339] \"We're here!\" Mrs. Swetnam said cheerfully, and with a blatant curiosity she looked around. Then she suddenly felt awkward and continued, \"I just thought I'd drop in and ask if you happened to want a kitten, Miss Blacklock? Our cat is about to\u2014\"\n[340] \"To be sent to the nest of a vigorous tomcat,\" Edmund said, \"and I think it will be terrible. I've warned you!\" [341] \"It's a wonderful mouser,\" Mrs. Sweetnam hastened to say, adding, \"The chrysanthemums are looking so well!\" [342] \"You're using a central heating system, aren't you?\" Edmund said with a tone of discovery. [343] \"Why do people all sound like gramophones?\" Julia muttered. [344] \"I don't like that news,\" Colonel Eastbrook said to Patrick, \"I don't like it at all. If you ask me, I'd say war is inevitable, absolutely inevitable.\" [345] \"I never pay attention to the news,\" Patrick replied to him. [346] The door opened again, and Mrs. Harmon walked in. [347] She pinned her tattered hat back, as if trying to create some sort of fashion effect, and also changed out of her usual cardigan into a rumpled, frayed-edged jacket. [348] \"Good afternoon, Miss Blacklock,\" she said brightly, \"Am I too late for the murder?\" [349] 3\n[350] Sighs of anticipation could be heard all around. Julia chuckled appreciatively, Patrick scowled, and Miss Blacklock smiled at the last guest. [351] \"Julian was furious that he couldn't come,\" said Mrs. Harmon. \"He loves murder. That's why his sermon last Sunday was so good\u2014though I shouldn't say so, since he's my husband\u2014but it was, wasn't it? Much better than usual. And it was all because of that book, 'The Hat Trick of Death.' Have you read it? The girl at Buzzer's Bookshop held it for me. The plot is so intriguing. You think you know who the murderer is, and then suddenly, the whole thing turns on its head, and there are four or five possible culprits. Well, I left the book in the study one day, and Julian picked it up while he was preparing his sermon, and he got so engrossed in it that he had to rush his sermon and just jot down what he wanted to say, without any of his usual erudite references\u2014and, of course, it was much better that way. Oh, dear, I'm going on and on. But you must tell me when the murder starts?\" [352] Miss Blacklock glanced at the clock on the mantelpiece. [353] \"If it's to start,\" she said cheerfully, \"it should be any moment now. It's only a minute to six thirty. Let's have a sherry while we wait.\" [354] Patrick nipped across the hall. [355] Miss Blacklock went to the table by the hall, where the cigarette box was. [356] \"I'd love a sherry,\" said Mrs. Harmon, \"but what do you mean by 'if'?\" [357] \"Well,\" said Miss Blacklock, \"I'm as much in the dark as you are. I don't know what -\"\n[358] Suddenly, the clock on the mantelpiece struck, and she fell silent. The chimes were sweet and everyone fell quiet, no one moved. [359] Everyone stared at the clock. [360] The chimes began at the quarter past six position and continued until they reached three-quarters past. And just as the last chime faded away, all the lights went out. [361] 4\n[362] In the darkness, there was only the sound of excited breathing and the murmurs of approval from the women. [363] \"It's begun,\" cried Mrs. Harmon, ecstatically. \"Oh, how detestable!\" moaned Dora Bunner. \"Good heavens!\" said others. \"It's awful! It's awful! It gives me gooseflesh. Where are you, Archibald? What am I supposed to do? Oh, dear--I've stepped on your foot. I'm terribly sorry.\" [364] Suddenly, there was a creak, and the door slid open. A beam of intense light flashed around the room. A man's voice, hoarse and nasal, echoed through the room, instantly reminding everyone of those pleasant afternoons spent in the cinema:\n[365] \"Hands up! I said, hands up!\" the man shouted. [366]Hands raised high and willingly over their heads. [367]\"Isn't this thrilling?\" a woman whispered, \"I'm so excited.\" [368]Unexpectedly, a revolver fired twice. The sound of the bullets shattered the joyful atmosphere in the room. Suddenly, it was no longer a game, and someone screamed...\n[369]The shadow at the door turned abruptly, seeming to hesitate for a moment. A third bullet was fired, and the shadow stumbled before collapsing to the ground. The flashlight fell, and the light disappeared. [370]Darkness descended again. Then, with a characteristic creak of Victorian-era craftsmanship, the living room door slid gently back into place, finally clicking shut. [371] 5\n[372] The living room was in chaos, and everyone spoke at once. [373] \"The light.\" [374] \"Can you find the switch?\" [375] \"Who has a lighter?\" [376]\"Oh, I hate this, I hate it!\" [377]\"But those gunshots were real!\" [378]\"He was using a real revolver.\" [379]\"Was it a burglar?\" [380]\"Ah, Arch, I want to leave here.\" [381] \"Who has a lighter, please?\" [382] Then, almost at the same time, two lighters clicked, producing a weak yet steady flame. [383] Everyone blinked and looked around, staring at each other in terror. Miss Blacklock leaned against the arcade wall, her hand covering her cheek. Only a faint outline of something dark could be seen trickling from between her fingers. [384] Colonel Eastbrook cleared his throat and stepped forward spontaneously. [385] \"Try the switch, Swetnam,\" he ordered. [386] Edmund, standing near the door, turned the switch up and down as instructed. [387] \"The main switch is off, or the fuse is blown,\" said the colonel. \"Who's making all the noise?\" [388] A woman's scream echoed from behind the closed door, growing louder and more shrill, accompanied by the sound of fists pounding on the door. [389] Dora Bunner had been quietly crying, and now she blurted out, \"It's Micky. Someone's trying to kill Micky...\"\n[390] \"No such luck,\" Patrick muttered. [391] \"We need to get some candles,\" said Miss Blacklock. \"Patrick, please\u2014\"\n[392] The Colonel was opening the door. He and Edmund, holding a flickering lighter, stepped into the hall and almost tripped over a body lying on the ground. [393] \"Looks like he's been knocked down,\" said the Colonel. \"Where's that screeching woman?\" [394] \"In the dining room,\" said Edmund. [395] The dining room was on the other side of the hall. Someone was pounding on the boards, shrieking. [396] \"She's locked in,\" Edmund said, bending down. He turned the key, and Midge burst out like a tiger out of thin air. [397] The dining room lights were still on. The light faintly illuminated Midge, who looked terrified and kept screaming. The amusing thing was that she had been washing silverware, so she still held a piece of deer skin and a large frying pan. [398] \"Quiet, Midge,\" Miss Blacklock said. [399] \"Stop screaming,\" Edmund said, but Midge didn't stop screaming, so he stepped forward and slapped her sharply. Midge gasped and choked, finally quieting down. [400] \"Get some candles,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"In the kitchen cabinet, Patrick. Do you know where the fuse box is?\" [401] \"Behind the dish cupboard, right? Okay, I'll see what I can do.\" [402] Miss Blacklock had already moved into the light from the restaurant. Dora Bunner gasped and choked back a sob, while Midge let out another piercing scream. [403] \"Blood, blood!\" she wailed. \"You're shot \u2013 Miss Blacklock, you're going to bleed to death.\" [404] \"Don't be silly,\" Miss Blacklock snapped. \"I'm not badly hurt, the bullet just grazed my ear.\" [405] \"But Aunt Letty,\" Julia said, \"all this blood.\" [406] Indeed, Miss Blacklock's blouse, pearl necklace, and hands were all covered in blood, looking rather ghastly. [407] \"Ears always bleed,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"I fainted in the barber's shop once when he cut my ear. Blood seemed to pour in a basin. But anyway, we must have light.\" [408] \"I'll get candles,\" Micky said. [409] Julia went with her, bringing back several candles stuck in dishes. [410] \"Now let's have a look at this villain,\" the Colonel said. \"Could you hold the candles lower, Swetnam? Bring as many as you can.\" [411] \"I'll go and light the other side,\" said Felicity. [412] She held two tea trays steady. The Colonel knelt down. [413] The prone figure wore a rough, hooded black cloak, a black mask over the face, and black cotton gloves. The hat had slipped back, revealing a tangled mass of golden hair. [414] Colonel Eastbrooke turned him over, feeling for a pulse, a heartbeat... and then