she never takes off, you'll find the scar from the operation.\" [2731] \"Surgery?\" [2732] \"Thyroidectomy.\" [2733] Miss Blacklock had calmed down and was now looking at Miss Marple. [2734] \"So you know everything?\" she said. [2735] \"Yes, for quite some time.\" [2736] Charlotte Blacklock sat down at the table and burst into tears. [2737] \"You shouldn't have done that,\" she said. \"You shouldn't have imitated Dora's voice. I love Dora. I really do love Dora.\" [2738] Inspector Edward and the others crowded around the door. [2739] Sergeant Edwards, with his varied skills and knowledge of first aid and artificial respiration, was busy with Midge, who, as soon as she could speak, began to gush self-congratulatory words. [2740] \"I did splendidly, didn't I? I'm so clever! And I'm so brave! Oh, I'm so brave! Brave enough to be almost killed. But I dared to risk my life.\" [2741] Miss Quincannon pushed past the people around her, leaping towards Charlotte Blacklock, who was sobbing at the table. [2742] Chief Inspector Fletcher had to use all his strength to pull her back. [2743] \"Enough,\" he said, \"enough\u2014no, no, Miss Quincannon\u2014\"\n[2744] Miss Quincannon spoke through gritted teeth, \"Let me go and finish her off! Don't stop me. It was her who killed Amy Muggleton.\" [2745] Charlotte Blacklock looked up and snorted. [2746] \"I didn't want to kill her. I didn't want to kill anyone\u2014I had to, but I care about Dora. I was all alone after she died, ever since she died. Oh, Dora, Dora\u2014\"\n[2747] She bowed her head and covered her face with her hands, sobbing. [2748] Chapter XXIII: The Parsonage\n[2749] Miss Marple sat in a high-backed armchair. Round sat on the floor in front of the fireplace, hugging her knees. [2750] Reverend Julian leaned forward, not looking like a mature man but like a schoolboy. Inspector Corcoran puffed on his pipe and sipped his whisky and soda, obviously relieved of his burden and at ease. Seated around them were Julia, Patrick, Edmund, and Felicia. [2751] \"I think it's your turn to tell the story, Miss Maple,\" Corcoran said. [2752] \"Oh, no, my dear child. I just chipped in a little bit here and there. You were the one in charge, you orchestrated everything, and you knew so much more than I did.\" [2753] \"Well, let's all chip in then,\" Round said impatiently. \"One person at a time. Let's start with Auntie Jan, because I like the way her brain works. When did you start to suspect that it was all a setup by Blacklock?\" [2754] \"Well, my dear Round, it's hard to say. Of course, from the beginning, it seemed like the ideal person to arrange the robbery, or at least the most conspicuous one, was Miss Blacklock herself. She was the only known person to have had contact with Rudi Scherz, and it would have been so easy to plan something like this in her own home. For example, turning off the central heating would have meant no need for a fire, which would have meant no light in the house. And only the house's owner could have made such an arrangement. [2755] \"I didn't always think this way - it's a shame things weren't simpler! Oh, no, I was fooled like everyone else, because I thought someone really wanted to kill Letitia Blacklock.\" [2756] \"I'd rather get to the bottom of what really happened,\" Round said. \"Did the Swiss boy recognize her?\" [2757] \"Yes. He worked at \u2013\"\n[2758] She hesitated, looking at Coraquil. [2759] \"In Bern, at Dr. Adolf Koch's clinic,\" Coraquil said. \"Koch was a world-renowned expert in thyroid surgery. Charlotte Blacklock went there to have her thyroid removed, and Rudi Scherz was a janitor. He recognized her when he came to England, and he struck up a conversation with her. I dare say, if he had thought about it, he wouldn't have done it, because he was sent away from home for misbehavior. However, it was some time after Charlotte left there, so she wouldn't have known about it.\" [2760] \"So, he didn't mention Monte Carlo and his father being the hotel owners?\" [2761] \"Ah, no, that was something she had to fabricate to explain why he spoke to her.\" [2762] \"Seeing him must have been a shock to her,\" said Miss Marple thoughtfully. \"She was safe, and then, due to an almost impossible coincidence, someone she knew appeared, not as one of the two Miss Blacklocks\u2014which she was prepared for\u2014but as Charlotte Blacklock, the patient who had had the thyroid operation. She was recognized, not as a guest at the hotel, but as the patient.\" [2763] \"Now, let me tell you from the beginning. I think\u2014if Inspector Kollarik agrees with me\u2014that it was Charlotte Blacklock, a beautiful, carefree, and loving girl who developed a thyroid enlargement. The illness ruined her life, as she was a sensitive girl and one who placed great importance on her appearance. And girls at that age are particularly sensitive about themselves. If she had a mother or a father who was understanding, I don't think she would have fallen into that state of mind, but unfortunately, she was suffering greatly. She had no one to take her out of her shell, to force her to socialize, and to make her lead a normal life, instead of fixating on her deformity. Of course, in another family, she might have had the surgery years ago. [2764] \"However, I think Dr. Blacklock was a conservative man, narrow-minded, cruel, and stubborn. He didn't believe in this surgery. Charlotte would have gotten the conclusion that she was powerless\u2014except for iodine and other drugs. Charlotte did believe him, and I think her sister trusted him too much as a physician. [2765] \"Charlotte expressed her loyalty to her father in a fragile and melancholic way, and she probably thought he was the most correct. She withdrew further into herself, and as a result, her thyroid continued to grow larger, and she became increasingly reclusive, refusing to see people. However, she was a kind and loving person at heart.\" [2766] \"It's strange to describe a murderer in that way,\" said Edmund. [2767] \"I don't agree,\" said Miss Marple. \"Weak and kind-hearted people are often the ones who betray most easily. Once they have a grudge against life, their moral strength is quickly drained away by it. [2768] \"Lettice Blacklock's character, however, was very different. Inspector Corcoran told me that Bell Goddard described her as too good, and I agree that Lettice was good. She was a moral person \u2013 in her own words \u2013 she couldn't understand why others couldn't see the dishonesty. She would never have the slightest thought of doing anything dishonest, no matter what the temptation. Lettice was very loyal to her sister. She wrote to her, telling her about everything that happened, trying to keep her in touch with life. She was very worried about Charlotte's mental state. [2769] \"Finally, Dr. Blacklock died. Lettice gave up her position at Randall Goddard's without hesitation and devoted her life to Charlotte. She took her to Switzerland to consult with authorities about the possibility of surgery. The surgery was too late, but we know it was successful. The deformity was removed, and the scars left by the operation were easily concealed with a string of pearls or a necklace. [2770] \"The end.\" [2771] \"Later, the war broke out, and the sisters found it difficult to return to England, so they stayed in Switzerland and worked for the Red Cross and other organizations. Is that correct, Inspector?\" [2772] \"Yes, Miss Marple.\" [2773] \"They occasionally heard news from England. I believe they also heard that Bell Goodale was not long for this world. I believe, purely out of human nature, they planned and discussed how they would spend the money after it was theirs. I think it must be recognized that for the sisters, this prospect meant more to Charlotte. For the first time in her life, Charlotte could feel like a normal woman and go anywhere without fear of being met with disgust or pity. She could finally enjoy life to the fullest, making up for lost time. She wanted to travel, buy a house and a beautiful garden, wear pretty clothes and sparkling jewelry, go to the theater and concert halls, and indulge every whim. For Charlotte, it was like a fairy tale coming true. [2774] \"However, Lady Lestrange, who was in good health, contracted the flu, which turned into pneumonia, and she died within a week. Charlotte not only lost her sister but also her dream. I think she almost resented Lady Lestrange for dying. They had just received a letter saying that Bell Goodale was not long for this world. At such a critical moment, why did Lady Lestrange have to die? Perhaps in a month, the money would have been Lady Lestrange's \u2013 once she died, it would have been hers\u2026\n[2775] \"At this point, I think the difference between the two became apparent. Charlotte did not feel that her thoughts were wrong \u2013 she thought there was nothing wrong with them. The money was meant for Lady Lestrange \u2013 it would have been hers in a few months \u2013 she saw herself and Lady Lestrange as the same person.\" [2776] \"Perhaps it was when someone asked her sister's Christian name that she had the idea. Suddenly, she realized that in the eyes of most people, the two Miss Blacklocks were indistinguishable - elderly, well-bred British women, dressed almost identically, with faces so similar that they were virtually identical. (I had pointed out to Roundell that older women look alike.) Why couldn't it have been Charlotte who died and Leticia who lived? [2777] \"I think it was more a matter of impulse than careful planning. Leticia was buried under Charlotte's name. 'Charlotte' died, and 'Leticia' returned to England. The creativity and energy that nature had given her had been dormant for years and now came to the fore. As Charlotte, she had been a minor character. Now she had a face that dominated others - the sense of dominance that belonged to Leticia. Their brains were not very different, I believe, but their moral differences were vast. [2778] \"Charlotte naturally took a few obvious measures. She bought a house in an unfamiliar place in England. She had to avoid only a few people from her hometown - she had lived a solitary life there - and Bella Goddard, who was too familiar with Leticia to pull off the switch. Despite her arthritic fingers, she managed to forge the handwriting. It was actually quite easy, as few people really knew Charlotte.\u201d\n[2779] \"What if she met someone who knew Leticia?\" Roundell asked. \"There must have been quite a few.\" [2780] \"They would not be a problem either. Someone might say, 'I met Leticia the other day. She's changed so much that I didn't recognize her.' But their minds would not doubt that it was not Leticia. Ten years can change a person. And she could always blame her poor eyesight for not recognizing them. You must remember that she knew all the details of Leticia's life in London, including the people she knew and the places she went. She could refer to the letters Leticia had written to her, mention some events, or ask about the circumstances of mutual friends, and quickly dispel any doubts. No, she was only afraid of being recognized as Charlotte.\" [2781] \"She settled down in Little Fulham and got to know the neighbors. Later, she received a letter asking for dear Letitia's help, and she happily accepted the visit of two young cousins she had never met. [2782] \"Everything proceeded flawlessly. It was at this point that she made a grave mistake. This mistake stemmed entirely from her compassionate nature and benevolent disposition. She received a letter from an old school friend who was down on her luck, so she went to help her. Perhaps part of the reason was that, despite having everything, she was lonely. Her secret had made her shun others. She had always fondly remembered Dorothy Bunner as a symbol of her carefree and happy days at school. Regardless, in a moment of impulse, she wrote a letter to Dorothy herself. Dorothy would have been overjoyed: she wrote to Letitia, and the reply was from her sister Charlotte. It would have been impossible to pretend to be Letitia with Dorothy. Dorothy was one of the few people introduced to her by Charlotte during her lonely and melancholy days. [2783] \"Because she knew Dorothy would speak her mind, she told her what she had done. Dorothy wholeheartedly agreed. In her muddled mind, she believed that Letit should not have been deprived of her inheritance because of Charlotte's death. Since Letit had bravely endured all the suffering, she deserved recompense. It would have been unfair if the money had fallen into the hands of someone who had never heard of her. [2784] \"She knew that this must be kept secret. It was like an extra pound of butter that was fine, but couldn't be talked about. So, Dorothy came to Little Fulham. And soon, Charlotte realized that she had made a terrible mistake. Not only was it due to Dorothy's poor eyesight and clumsy hands, which caused many mistakes and drove people crazy when living with her, but also because Dorothy became a real crisis. Although Charlotte and Letitia called each other by their full names, Dorothy was always using nicknames. And although she had learned to call her friend Letit, the old name often slipped out of her mouth. In addition, memories of the past were easily brought up - Charlotte had to constantly keep her from accidentally blurting out something due to forgetfulness. This began to make her anxious. [2785] \"However, it was unlikely that anyone\n[2786] \"But she might have a different opinion. She might think it was a despicable blackmail, suspecting that he might have discovered something about her and that, once the news of Bella Goddle's death was published in the newspapers, he might realize that he had found a gold mine on her. [2787] \"Now she was determined to deceive. She had appeared as Letty Blacklock, using that identity both for the bank and for Mrs. Goddle. The only unexpected obstacle was the rather dubious Swiss hotel waiter, whose character was far from reliable and who might be a fraud. Once he was out of the way, she could rest easy. [2788] \"Perhaps she initially conceived this plan as a fantasy. She had experienced the cravings of love and drama in her life, so she delightedly elaborated on the details. So, how could she get rid of him? [2789] \"Now she was determined to deceive. She had appeared as Letty Blacklock, using that identity both for the bank and for Mrs. Goddle. The only unexpected obstacle was the rather dubious Swiss hotel waiter, whose character was far from reliable and who might be a fraud. Once he was out of the way, she could rest easy. [2790] \"Perhaps she initially conceived this plan as a fantasy. She had experienced the cravings of love and drama in her life, so she delightedly elaborated on the details. So, how could she get rid of him?\" [2791] \"She formulated her plan and ultimately decided to put it into action. She told Rudi Scherz the story of playing a robbery game at the party, explaining that a stranger would play the role of the 'thief,' and promising him a large sum of money for his cooperation.\" [2792] \"He agreed without hesitation, which further convinces me that Scherz did not have any leverage over her. To him, she was just a foolish old lady willing to part with her money.\" [2793] \"She gave him the ad to place in the newspaper, arranged for him to visit the small estate to study the layout of the grounds, and showed him the meeting place - where she would pick him up on the night of the murder and lead him into the house. Of course, Miss Bunner had no idea about any of this.\" [2794] \"Then, the day came -\" he paused. [2795] Miss Marple continued in her gentle voice. [2796] \"She must have been very nervous that day. You see, it was not too late to turn back... Dora Bunner told us that Letitia was very frightened that day, and of course she was. She was afraid of what she was about to do, afraid that the plan would go wrong, but not afraid enough to turn back.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:08 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nAnd this long-handled deep frying pan, this pan, I only use it to boil milk\u2014\"\n[2618] \"Well, I don't know which pan you use for what,\" Julia said angrily, \"you should go to bed, why are you getting up again? I can't even imagine. Get out of my way, let me wash the dishes in peace.\" [2619] \"No, you can't use my kitchen.\" [2620] \"Oh, Midge, you're unbearable!\" [2621] Julia angrily stormed out of the kitchen just as the doorbell rang. [2622] \"I'm not going to answer the door!\" Micky called from the kitchen. Julia muttered a European swear word and strode to the front door. [2623] It was Miss Quincannon. [2624] \"Good evening,\" she croaked, \"I'm sorry to barge in again. I assume the inspector called, right?\" [2625] \"He didn't tell us you were coming,\" Julia said, leading the guest into the living room. [2626] \"He said I didn't have to come unless I wanted to,\" Miss Chicliff said. \"But I very much wanted to.\" [2627] No one offered Miss Chicliff any sympathy for her situation or mentioned Miss Muggleton's death. The tall, robust woman, with a look of relief on her face, made any words of pity seem inadequate. [2628] \"Turn all the lights on,\" Miss Blacklock said. \"Put some more coal on the fire. I'm so cold\u2014so very cold. Come and sit by the fire, Miss Chicliff. The Inspector said he'd be here in fifteen minutes, and it's about time now.\" [2629] \"Micki's coming down again,\" Julia said. [2630] \"Is she? Sometimes I think the girl is mad\u2014mad as a hatter. But perhaps we're all mad.\" [2631] \"I can't stand the idea of all criminals being mad,\" exclaimed Miss Quincannon indignantly. \"To me, they're all sane\u2014even clever\u2014in a queer way.\" [2632] There was a sound of a car coming up, and presently Colonel Cadorick, with the Captain and Mrs. Sweeney and the Sweeney children, entered. They all looked very grave. Then Colonel Cadorick lowered his voice and said: \"Oh! Oh! It's getting warm in here!\" [2633] Mrs. Cadorick made an effort to liven up the atmosphere, and her efforts were almost grotesque. [2634] \"Terrible, isn't it?\" she said. \"I mean all this. You know, one slip of the tongue and you never know who's going to be next\u2014just like the plague.\" [2635] \"Mummy,\" said Edmund in an agony of patience, \"can't you stop talking?\" [2636] \"I promise, dear, I won't say another word,\" Mrs. Swetnam said, then sat down on the couch next to Julia. Constable Colquhoun stood near the door, facing a row of three women \u2013 Julia and Mrs. Swetnam on the couch, and Mrs. Eustace on the arm of her husband's chair. It was not intentional, but the arrangement worked perfectly. [2637] Miss Blacklock and Miss Quincannon were bent over the fire. Edmund stood nearby, and Philipa was in the shadows at the back. [2638] Constable Colquhoun began bluntly, \"As you all know, Miss Mulgrave was murdered. We have reason to believe that the killer is a woman. For other reasons, we can narrow down the suspect pool. I would like to ask each of you ladies what you were doing from 4:00 PM to 4:20 PM today. I have already heard from the young lady who claims to be Miss Simmons. I would like her to repeat what she said. Meanwhile, Miss Simmons, I must remind you that if you believe your answer could incriminate you, you do not have to answer. Everything you say will be recorded by Detective Edwards.\" [2639] \"Do you really have to say that?\" Julia asked. Her face was exceptionally pale, but her demeanor was calm. \"I repeat, from 4:00 PM to 4:30 PM, I was walking along the field next to the stream leading to the farmhouse at Compton. I walked back to the road through the field with three white poplars. As far as I can remember, I did not encounter anyone. I did not approach the Gravel Pit House.\" [2640] \"Mrs. Swetnam?\" [2641] Edmund asked: \"Is your warning for all of us?\" [2642] The superintendent turned to him. [2643] \"No. At present, it is only Miss Simmons. I have no reason to believe that what anyone else says will incriminate themselves, but anyone, of course, has the right to have a lawyer present and refuse to answer questions when the lawyer is not present.\" [2644] \"Oh, it's so stupid and completely wasted time,\" Mrs. Sweetnam said loudly. \"I swear I can tell you what I was doing at that time. That's what you want, isn't it? Can I start now?\" [2645] \"Yes, please, Mrs. Sweetnam.\" [2646] \"Now let me think.\" Mrs. Swetnam closed her eyes, then opened them again, \"Of course, I have nothing to do with Miss Mulgrew's murder, and I'm sure everyone here knows that. However, I'm a person who understands the ways of the world, and I know that the police have to ask some of the most unnecessary questions and write down the answers very carefully, because it's all for what they call 'records,' isn't it?\" [2647] Mrs. Swetnam suddenly asked this question to Detective Edwards, who was an excellent stenographer but lacked the art of diplomacy. His face turned red to his ears, and he replied, \"No problem, ma'am. Maybe a little slower would be better.\" [2648] Mrs. Swetnam continued her long speech and made clear pauses where she thought commas or periods were appropriate. [2649] \"Of course, it's hard to be exact, because my sense of time isn't very strong. Since the war, half of our clocks don't work at all, and the other half, because they're not wound up, are either fast or slow or don't work at all.\" Mrs. Swetnam stopped, letting everyone absorb this picture of chaos in timekeeping, and then continued sincerely, \"I think I was darning my socks at four o'clock \u2013 because of some unusual circumstances, I had reversed the direction \u2013 using silver and gold thread, you know, not plain white cloth \u2013 but if I hadn't been doing that, I would have been outside trimming the dead flowers \u2013 no, that was earlier, before the rain.\" [2650] \"That rain,\" the Inspector said, \"started at four-fifteen.\" [2651] \"Really? That's helpful. Of course, I was upstairs at the time, with a basin in the passage to catch the rain, which always leaks there, and the rain comes in so fast that I immediately guessed the gutter must be blocked again. So I came down to put on my raincoat and rubber boots. I called Edmund, but he didn't answer, so I thought he must have been writing at the crucial part of his novel, and I didn't bother him. Besides, I often do it myself. You know, with a broom, a broomstick tied to a long stick to push up the window.\" [2652] \"You mean,\" noticed Corcoran, seeing his subordinate's puzzled expression, \"you were cleaning the gutter?\" [2653] \"Yes, it was all blocked up with leaves. It took me a long time, and I got quite wet, but I finally cleared it. Then I went home to change \u2013 the smell of the dead leaves was awful. Then I went to the kitchen, put the kettle on the fire.\" [2654] Detective Edwards blinked. [2655] \"That means,\" Mrs. Swetnam concluded triumphantly, \"that the actual time was twenty to five.\" [2656] \"Or very close,\" she added. [2657] \"Did anyone see you outside cleaning the gutter?\" [2658] \"No, not at all,\" Mrs. Sweetnam said. \"If there had been anyone, I would have called him to help! It's so hard to do it alone.\" [2659] \"So, according to your statement, you were outside in the rain wearing a raincoat and galoshes, and you were cleaning the gutter, but you have no one to vouch for that?\" [2660] \"You can see the gutter for yourself,\" Mrs. Sweetnam said. \"It's spotless.\" [2661] \"Did you hear your mother call you, Mr. Swetnam?\" [2662] \"No,\" Edmund answered. \"I was sleeping soundly.\" [2663] \"Edmund,\" his mother chided. \"I thought you were writing.\" [2664] Inspector Corrado turned to Mrs. Eastbrook: \"Your turn, Mrs. Eastbrook.\" [2665] \"I was sitting in Arch's study with him,\" Mrs. Eastbrook answered, looking at him with innocent eyes. \"We were listening to the radio, right, Arch?\" [2666] There was a brief pause. Colonel Eastbrook blushed and took his wife's hand. [2667] \"You don't understand these things, kitty,\" he said. \"I\u2014well\u2014I must say, Inspector, you've taken us quite by surprise. My wife, you know, has been very upset by all this. She's on edge, very tense, and she doesn't understand the importance of\u2014considering\u2014before making a statement.\" [2668] \"Archibald,\" Mrs. Eastbrook reprimanded him. \"Are you going to say you weren't with me?\" [2669] \"No, right? I mean, one must be truthful. It's very important in these interrogations. I was talking to Mr. Lumpson, the farmer from Crofton, about how to make money from chickens. It was a quarter to four. I came home just as the rain stopped, just before tea, at a quarter to five. Laura was baking scones.\" [2670] \"So you were out, too, Mrs. Eastbrook?\" [2671] Her pretty face became more and more like a weasel's, her eyes had a trapped look. [2672] \"No - no, I was just sitting and listening to the radio, I didn't go out. Not then. I went out earlier, about - about half past three, just for a little walk, not far.\" [2673] She looked expectant for more questions, but Corradini said calmly, \"That's all, Mrs. Eastbrook.\" [2674] He continued, \"The statement will be typed out. You can look it over, and if it's correct, please sign it.\" [2675] Mrs. Eastbrook glared at him suddenly. [2676] \"Why don't you ask others where they were? Like that woman, Hammers? Edmund Sweeney? How do you know he was really sleeping in the house? No one saw him.\" [2677] Chief Inspector Corrado said calmly, \"Miss McGarrett said some things before she died. On the night of the robbery that happened in this house, someone wasn't here at the time. Miss McGarrett told her friend the names of the people she saw in the room. By eliminating one by one, she found that there was one person she didn't see.\" [2678] \"No one could see anything,\" Julia said. [2679] \"Miss McGarrett could,\" Miss Chilcote suddenly said in a deep voice, \"she was behind the door, right where Chief Inspector Corrado is standing now. She was the only one who saw everything that happened.\" [2680] \"Ah-ha! That's your imagination, isn't it?\" Mickey asked. [2681] She dramatically entered, slamming the door and pushing Coolidge aside, unusually excited. [2682] \"Oh, you don't let Mickey come in with others, do you, you stiff policeman! I'm just Mickey, the kitchen's Mickey! Let her stay in the kitchen! She belongs to the kitchen! But I'll tell you, Mickey sees as well as others, maybe even clearer. Yes, I saw something on the night of the robbery, and I'm convinced of it, and I haven't said it until now. I thought, I won't tell what I saw until the time is right, I'll wait.\" [2683] \"So you're going to ask someone for some money, huh?\" Coolidge said. [2684] Mickey turned to him, looking like an angry cat. [2685] \"Why not? Why do you look down on people? Since I've been so generous in keeping silent, why shouldn't I get paid? Especially when there's going to be money - a lot of money. Ah! I heard - I know what's going on. I know this 'Pip Emma' - this one -\" she suddenly pointed a finger at Julia, \"the secret society that's acting as a spy inside.\" Yes, I could have waited for the money - but now I'm afraid. I'd rather be safe. Because maybe soon someone will kill me. So I'm going to say what I know.\" [2686] \"Well, okay,\" the Inspector said suspiciously, \"what do you know?\" [2687] \"I'll tell you,\" Midge said solemnly, \"that night I wasn't in the silverware room cleaning silver, when I heard the gunshot, I was already in the dining room. I peered through the keyhole, the hallway was dark, but the gunshot was loud, and the flashlight fell to the ground - I saw her. I saw her with a gun in her hand, right next to him. I saw Miss Blacklock.\" [2688] \"Me?\" Miss Blacklock jumped up from her seat, \"you must be crazy!\" [2689] \"But that's impossible,\" Edmund called out, \"Midge couldn't have seen Miss Blacklock.\" [2690] Coladock suddenly interrupted him, his voice acidic and sarcastic. [2691] \"Why not? Just because the woman holding the gun wasn't Miss Blacklock? So it was you, wasn't it?\" [2692] \"I \u2013 certainly not \u2013 damn it \u2013\"\n[2693] \"It was you who stole Colonel Eastbrook's revolver. It was you who conspired with Rudi Schlesinger \u2013 to pull off a joke. You followed Patrick Simmons into the little drawing room, and when the lights went out, you slipped out through the oiled door. You shot Miss Blacklock, and then you killed Rudi Schlesinger. A few seconds later, you came back into the drawing room, striking a match.\" [2694] For a moment, Edmund seemed at a loss for words, then he blurted out, \"The whole idea is too horrible to contemplate. Why me? What motive could I have?\" [2695] \"If Miss Blacklock had died before Mrs. Goodale, remember, there would have been two people to inherit the legacy. We only know them as Pip and Emma. Julia Simmons was really Emma \u2013\"\n[2696] \"And you think I'm Pip?\" Edmund laughed heartily. \"What a ridiculous idea \u2014 utterly ridiculous! I'm just about the right age \u2014 that's all. I can prove to you, you damned fool, that I'm Edmund Swetnam. Birth certificate, school certificates, university degrees \u2014 everything.\" [2697] \"He's not Pip.\" A voice came from the shadows in the corner. Philippa Hemmings stepped forward, pale-faced. \"I am Pip, Inspector.\" [2698] \"You, Mrs. Hemmings?\" [2699] \"Yes. Everyone seems to think Pip is a boy \u2014 of course, Julia knew her twin was a girl, but I didn't know why she didn't say this afternoon \u2014\"\n[2700] \"For family harmony,\" Julia said. \"I suddenly realized who you were. But up until then, I didn't know.\" [2701] \"I agree with Julia,\" Philippa said, her voice trembling slightly. \"After losing my husband and the end of the war, I didn't know what to do. My mother died many years ago. I discovered our Goodel family's relatives. Mrs. Goodel was dying, and when she passed away, the money would fall into the hands of Miss Blacklock. I found out where Miss Blacklock lived, so I\u2014I came here. I found a job at Mrs. Lucas' house. I hoped, since Miss Blacklock was an old lady and had no relatives, she might be willing to help me. Not for me\u2014because I can work\u2014but to help Harry's education. After all, it's Goodel family money, and she has no particular relatives to spend it on.\" [2702] \"Later,\" Philippa spoke faster, as if the floodgates of long-held emotions had suddenly opened, \"after the robbery, I began to feel afraid. Because it seemed to me that the only person who could have a motive to kill Miss Blacklock was me. I had no idea which one was Emma\u2014we're not identical twins, and we don't look alike at all. So, it seemed that I was the only one who should be suspected.\" [2703] She paused, brushing her hair back from her face. Coleridge suddenly realized that the faded snapshot in the letterbox must be Philippa's mother. The resemblance was undeniable. He also understood why the phrase \"hands repeatedly clenching and unclenching\" sounded so familiar\u2014Philippa was doing it right now. [2704] \"Miss Blacklock treated me very well, very well\u2014I never intended to kill her, and I never had such a thought. But it's still the same, I am Pip.\" She added, \"You see, you don't have to suspect Edmund anymore.\" [2705] \"No need?\" Coleridge said, his voice again with that biting tone. \"Edmund Swetnam is a man who loves money. A young man, perhaps wanting to marry a rich woman. But if Miss Blacklock didn't die before Mrs. Goodel, the woman he wanted to marry wouldn't be rich. Since it's almost certain that Mrs. Goodel will die before Miss Blacklock, Swetnam, isn't it, has to do something, isn't it?\" [2706] \"This is all damned lies!\" Edmund shouted. [2707] Just then, there was a sudden cry, coming from the kitchen - a long, terrifying scream of fear. [2708] \"It's not Midge!\" Julia exclaimed. [2709] \"No,\" Inspector Corrado said, \"it's the killer who murdered three people\u2026\"\n[2710] Chapter 22: The Truth Revealed\n[2711] As the inspector turned his attention to Edmund Sweeney, Midge slipped out of the living room and back into the kitchen. She was filling the sink with water when Miss Blacklock suddenly walked in. [2712] Midge felt too ashamed to look her in the eye. [2713] \"You really know how to lie, Midge,\" Miss Blacklock said cheerfully. \"Here \u2013 dishes aren't washed like this. First, you fill the sink with water. You can't wash anything with just two inches of water.\" [2714] Midge obediently turned on the faucet again.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."